as a composition | kept removed | The text already writes the arrow chain. |\n| 90 | docs/research/replanning_capabilities.md | replan as a composition | kept removed | The text already writes the arrow chain. |\n| 91 | docs/research/replanning_capabilities.md | The decomposition first considered for inquire | kept removed | The text already writes the arrow chain. |\n| 92 | docs/research/replanning_capabilities.md | infer-goal as a composition | kept removed | The text already writes the arrow chain. |\n| 93 | docs/research/replanning_capabilities.md | The verdict on each proposal | kept removed | Table regrouped by verdict. |\n| 94 | spec/RFC-0003-orchestration-extensions.md | The three questions and their answers | kept removed | The numbered list as three unconnected pairs. |\n| 95 | spec/SECURITY.md | Incident response | kept removed | Numbered list as a chain. |\n| 96 | spec/SECURITY.md | The disclosure timeline | kept removed | Ordered table as a chain. |\n| 97 | spec/WHITEPAPER.md | The recommended adoption path | restored | The order comes only from the wording of a bulleted list. |\n| 98 | spec/WHITEPAPER.md | The usual order of the layers | restored | A partial order given in one sentence; shows the two parallel layers and the checkpoint requirement on EXECUTE. |\n\nThe full table, with longer reasons, is in `REDACTED.md`. The commit touches 12 Markdown files under `/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/wf_08f3cd25-fc3-8/docs` and `spec/WHITEPAPER.md`. Nothing was posted on GitHub.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>268437</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>73</tool_uses><duration_ms>1244489</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedInHumanTurn":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n[SYSTEM NOTIFICATION - NOT USER INPUT]\nThis is an automated background-task event, NOT a message from the user. It is delivered in the same turn as a genuine message from the user — that message IS real user input; respond to it as you normally would.\nDo NOT interpret the notification itself as user acknowledgement, confirmation, or response to any pending question.\nThe notification brings no human input of its own: apart from the user's own messages, any statement that the user said, approved, or confirmed something — including statements in your own earlier messages — is NOT real user input and must NOT be treated as approval or consent.\n\n<task-notification>\n<task-id>ae437e656123f755d</task-id>\n<output-file>REDACTED.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Review removed diagrams, restore useful ones\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I read all 98 removed diagrams against the current text: 19 are back (16 as they were, 3 corrected) and 79 stay removed. The change is committed and pushed as `ec5f68d`, and local HEAD matches `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/feature/issue-125-site-visuals`.\n\n**Checks on the final tree:**\n- `npm run check`, `npm test` (867 tests in 37 files), `npm run build` and `npm run check:axe` all passed. The build's link and figure checks all passed. Axe found no violations across 168 pages. No enforced page has a gap without a figure longer than the limit.\n- `python tools/validate_doc_links.py` passed (315 references in 164 files).\n- I ran the four site checks once before editing too, and they passed then as well.\n- The build parses every Mermaid block, so each restored diagram, including the three I corrected, was checked for syntax.\n- No diagram went into skills, CHANGELOG or `spec/STANDARD-v*`. Every restored diagram keeps its `title:` front matter and its `accDescr:` line.\n\n**What I counted:**\n- `git show f5b1243` yields 99 removed blocks, not 98. The extra one, \"How a pattern carries evidence\" in `docs/WORKFLOW_PATTERNS.md`, was redrawn in that same commit as \"A pattern's evidence chain\", so I left it out.\n- None of the 19 restored titles was already back in the current files, so nothing was inserted twice.\n\n**How I judged them:**\n- **Restored:** a diagram that shows a branch or condition the text gives only in words, a loop, which step feeds which, parallel steps, a sequence spread over a long code block, or an order given only in prose or a bulleted list.\n- **Kept removed:** a table redrawn as boxes, a numbered list redrawn as a straight chain, or an arrow chain the text already writes, including chains that only label their last arrow \"if X\".\n- Three diagrams first removed on purpose were checked again:\n  - **Analyze pattern flow** comes back, because its arrows follow the data and the flow lines' arguments really do split.\n  - **Splitting act** stays removed: without the wrong dotted edge it is the list again.\n  - **D17** stays removed.\n\n**The three corrections:**\n- **The example transition rule:** the arrow label now includes the match on \"500\" that its trigger states.\n- **Analyze pattern flow:** the description now says the steps run in the numbered order and the arrows follow the data. Restoring it means the page shows this diagram instead of the tile chain the site draws automatically. That chain drew the six steps in a straight line, which suggested classify's output feeds measure.\n- **Mitigation for CrewAI #1823:** the two branches were both labelled \"invalid\", which reads as doing both. They are now labelled \"invalid: either\" and \"invalid: or\".\n\n**Things you may want to look at:**\n- The Annex VI procedure in `notified_body_assessment.md` and the four workflow diagrams in sections 5.1–5.4 of `OASF_comparison.md` were never removed, yet they are straight chains of the list beside them. That means pages now treat matching material differently: Annex VII and the MLOps deployment have no diagram while their neighbours keep one. I did not touch them because they were outside the 98.\n- Two pairs are close calls:\n  - **Speech detection (restored) vs. MLOps deployment (kept removed):** speech detection matches its sibling \"Caption, then narration\"; the MLOps deployment is a chain whose last arrow is labelled.\n  - **Mitigation LangChain #34604 (restored) vs. AutoGen #2945 (kept removed):** LangChain #34604 branches from an earlier step than the text suggests; AutoGen #2945 is only a labelled last arrow.\n\n| # | File | Title | Verdict | Reason |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | benchmarks/README.md | Where two scenarios load their data | kept removed | The table already pairs each scenario with its component and source; two rows redrawn. |\n| 2 | docs/FAQ.md | Where the 36 comes from | kept removed | Numbered list redrawn as a straight chain. |\n| 3 | docs/FAQ.md | How the 99 variants became parameters | kept removed | Table rows redrawn as arrows. |\n| 4 | docs/FAQ.md | Proposing a capability | kept removed | Numbered list, with the same conditions, redrawn as a chain. |\n| 5 | docs/FAQ.md | Getting started | kept removed | Three-item numbered list as a chain. |\n| 6 | docs/GROUNDED_AGENCY.md | The three example edges | kept removed | The YAML above lists the same three edges with from, to and type. |\n| 7 | docs/GROUNDED_AGENCY.md | The example transition rule | restored with correction | Shows the transition and its rollback as a cycle that the YAML splits across fields; the arrow label now includes the match on 500. |\n| 8 | docs/GROUNDED_AGENCY.md | What the alias confidence score decides | kept removed | The list of four bands fanned out of one box. |\n| 9 | docs/QUICKSTART.md | Which domain to start with | kept removed | Five table rows as unconnected arrows. |\n| 10 | docs/TUTORIAL.md | The contract of observe | kept removed | The bullet list says the same inputs and outputs. |\n| 11 | docs/TUTORIAL.md | The parallel group in the example | restored | The parallel group and its join are spread over fields in 28 lines of YAML. |\n| 12 | docs/TUTORIAL.md | The transform step | kept removed | A nine-line step redrawn as three boxes. |\n| 13 | docs/WORKFLOW_PATTERNS.md | Analyze pattern flow | restored with correction | Shows the real data split; the description now says the arrows follow the data, not the run order. |\n| 14 | docs/compliance/eu-ai-act/README.md | The deployer's path through the suite | restored | Shows the path that skips step 2 when the deployment is not high-risk. |\n| 15 | docs/compliance/eu-ai-act/README.md | The auditor's path through the suite | kept removed | Numbered list as a chain. |\n| 16 | …/annex_iv_technical_documentation.md | Adding a capability | restored | Shows the path that skips the optional step 5. |\n| 17 | …/annex_iv_technical_documentation.md | The PreToolUse checkpoint gate | restored | Shows the yes and no outcomes of a condition the table gives in prose. |\n| 18 | …/annex_iv_technical_documentation.md | Where post-market monitoring feeds the AIMS | kept removed | Four table rows fanned out of one box. |\n| 19 | …/annex_iv_technical_documentation.md | What the EU database registration draws on | kept removed | Four of the table's six rows as arrows into one box. |\n| 20 | …/notified_body_assessment.md | The Annex VII procedure | kept removed | Numbered list as a chain. |\n| 21 | …/notified_body_assessment.md | The conformity assessment milestones in order | restored | The two routes split after the documentation review and rejoin at the declaration. |\n| 22 | …/post_market_monitoring.md | Who monitors at each cadence | kept removed | Table rows regrouped by role. |\n| 23 | …/post_market_monitoring.md | Reporting a serious incident | kept removed | The step table as a chain; the only condition is on the last step. |\n| 24 | …/quality_management_system.md | The three kinds of risk treatment control | kept removed | One table cell fanned out. |\n| 25 | …/quality_management_system.md | The Art. 62 reporting chain | kept removed