g type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 127:62] Parameter 'd' implicitly has an 'any' type. [7006] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 130:36] Parameter 'acc' implicitly has an 'any' type. [7006] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 130:41] Parameter 'r' implicitly has an 'any' type. [7006] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 130:44] Parameter 'i' implicitly has an 'any' type. [7006] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 131:40] Parameter 'step' implicitly has an 'any' type. [7006] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 131:46] Parameter 'i' implicitly has an 'any' type. [7006] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 136:7] Type '{} | null' is not assignable to type 'string | null'.\n  Type '{}' is not assignable to type 'string'. [2322] (typescript)\n\nindex-figures.ts:\n  ✘ [Line 4:30] Cannot find module './repo' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 5:29] Cannot find module './rfcs' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 6:29] Cannot find module './status' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 7:34] Cannot find module './views' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n\nspec-dsl-figures.ts:\n  ✘ [Line 11:37] Cannot find module 'mdast' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 12:26] Cannot find module 'mdast-util-to-string' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 13:23] Cannot find module 'yaml' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)\n  ✘ [Line 14:50] Cannot find module '../data/diagnostics' or its corresponding type declarations. [2307] (typescript)</new-diagnostics>"}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"6e710bfae58656a0e9211be067aee3fbf0b480403a94b3fdc60d52f570934ad1","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The rollout run passed at commit 4a1ccd1 and is pushed; I fixed one reversed link direction in the capability-page neighbour check and am reviewing the output. Four changes need your approval before I send screenshots.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FG97s7mLy5H19N6gx6y3Uf","name":"AskUserQuestion","input":{"questions":[{"question":"mutate's skill text said it \"commonly follows verify\", but the ontology says verify runs after mutate, so a figure on the page contradicted another figure. The rollout run changed the skill text (skills/mutate/SKILL.md, outside the site) to say constrain. Keep that edit?","header":"mutate text","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Keep the edit (Recommended)","description":"The skill text now agrees with the ontology. A new test checks every skill's 'commonly follows / precedes' text against the ontology; mutate was the only one that disagreed."},{"label":"Revert, note it on the page","description":"Leave the skill text as it was, and have the figure say that the skill text and the ontology disagree."}]},{"question":"The design spec let some icons share a drawing: \"runs only if\" with \"has gates\", \"approval\" with \"required\", composed skill with nested field, and the Edge types card with the enables mark. The review found readers could not tell these apart, so each now has its own icon, and required schema files carry an asterisk instead of the approval stamp. Keep the separate icons?","header":"Icons","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Keep separate icons (Recommended)","description":"Each icon means one thing across the site. A test keeps the new pairs visually apart."},{"label":"Go back to the spec","description":"Restore the shared drawings the spec listed."}]},{"question":"The build now fails any page with more than two screens of text and no figure. Three stretches were exempted by judgement, not by the spec's list: rollback's Intent section (rollback has too few inputs and procedure matches for a figure), six SDK guide sections that are code blocks with one-line lead-ins, and the overview's five tables of document links. How should these be handled?","header":"Exemptions","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Keep the exemptions (Recommended)","description":"Each stretch is code or link tables with nothing to draw, or a capability with too little data. They are listed in one map that the build checks."},{"label":"Draw figures instead","description":"No new exemptions: find something from the data to draw in each stretch, even if it is thin."}]},{"question":"The new procedure figure on capability pages shows which inputs and outputs each step names. It matches field names as plain words, so the word \"data\" in a step counts as naming the `data` field. Matching only names written as code would give enough matches for a figure on 6 of 36 pages instead of 34. The figure's title and caption say it is a word match. Which do you want?","header":"Step figure","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Keep word matching (Recommended)","description":"34 of 36 pages get the figure; its caption states that a mark is a word match, so a reader knows it can over-count."},{"label":"Code names only","description":"Stricter and never wrong, but only 6 pages get the figure; the other 30 need something else to fill the gap."},{"label":"Remove the figure","description":"Drop it; the gaps it fills need other figures."}]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"d036782bbe3062fe77d44cebd339a9e818429e20df5370dc85702224e31db788","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","content":"Your questions have been answered: \"mutate's skill text said it \"commonly follows verify\", but the ontology says verify runs after mutate, so a figure on the page contradicted another figure. The rollout run changed the skill text (skills/mutate/SKILL.md, outside the site) to say constrain. Keep that edit?\"=\"Keep the edit (Recommended)\", \"The design spec let some icons share a drawing: \"runs only if\" with \"has gates\", \"approval\" with \"required\", composed skill with nested field, and the Edge types card with the enables mark. The review found readers could not tell these apart, so each now has its own icon, and required schema files carry an asterisk instead of the approval stamp. Keep the separate icons?\"=\"Keep separate icons (Recommended)\", \"The build now fails any page with more than two screens of text and no figure. Three stretches were exempted by judgement, not by the spec's list: rollback's Intent section (rollback has too few inputs and procedure matches for a figure), six SDK guide sections that are code blocks with one-line lead-ins, and the overview's five tables of document links. How should these be handled?\"=\"Keep the exemptions (Recommended)\", \"The new procedure figure on capability pages shows which inputs and outputs each step names. It matches field names as plain words, so the word \"data\" in a step counts as naming the `data` field. Matching only names written as code would give enough matches for a figure on 6 of 36 pages instead of 34. The figure's title and caption say it is a word match. Which do you want?\"=\"Keep word matching (Recommended)\". You can now continue with these answers in mind.","tool_use_id":"toolu_01FG97s7mLy5H19N6gx6y3Uf"}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"09a2bc296c65644202675e9a04bbf1fbef3ee2abd6d93ff36c7b5ab2fadf331f","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"You've approved all four items, so I'm moving on to the remaining prose: diagrams for the long documents, a sitewide check that every figure has matching Markdown text, and an independent review of the thin-data pages. I'll send the screenshots afterward.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01JEFyiHHHmG6B2hda8triaD","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'site-visual-round3',\n  description: 'Issue #125 round 3: Mermaid on remaining prose pages, sitewide figure/Markdown audit, independent review, fix',\n  phases: [{ title: 'Build' }, { title: 'Integrate' }, { title: 'Review' }, { title: 'Fix' }],\n}\nconst SP = 'REDACTED'\nconst WT = '/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/wf_08f3cd25-fc3-8'\nconst COMMON = `Repository synaptiai/agent-capability-standard. Integration worktree ${WT}, branch feature/issue-125-site-visuals, head 045c0cc (pushed except 045c0cc; do not push from a builder).\nRead first: ${SP}/rollout-brief.md (rules, tools, decisions), ${SP}/visual-build-brief.md (hard rules, commit format with git commit -F, no force-push, no GitHub posts, no PRs, no merge to main, nothing from synapti.ai), ${SP}/visual-spec.md (design spec incl. the amendments section at the end).\nThe maintainer has also decided (2026-11-12): keep the skills/mutate/SKILL.md edit; keep separate icons for each concept; keep the rollback / SDK-guide / overview exemptions; keep the word-match procedure figure.\nReuse the existing Mermaid renderer (site/src/lib/mermaid/, flowchart subset, accDescr required) and shared figure parts. Run npm run check, npm test, npm run build and npm run check:axe before reporting; all must pass. Open (Read) the screenshots you take before you report.`\n\nconst BUILDERS = [\n  { name: 'prose-a', task: `Add Mermaid diagrams written into the Markdown source of: docs/GLOSSARY.md, docs/FAQ.md (sections beyond the three diagrams already there), docs/QUICKSTART.md, docs/TUTORIAL.md, every file under docs/research/ and docs/methodology/ that the site renders, spec/CHANGELOG.md and CHANGELOG.md only where an entry describes a structure, flow or sequence, spec/SECURITY*.md / spec/CONFORMANCE*.md / spec/EXTENDING* if routed. Rule: a diagram goes only where the surrounding text describes a structure, a flow or a sequence, and it says only what that text says, with an accDescr: line. Do not invent content to fill a gap. For each page, report its longest run before and after (node scripts/check-runs.mjs --all against a built dist, ACS_RUNS_ENFORCE=0), and for each page still over 1,800px name the section and say concretely why nothing in it is a structure, flow or sequence. No Mermaid in spec/STANDARD-v*.md.` },\n  { name: 'prose-b', task: `Add Mermaid diagrams written into the Markdown source of: every routed file under docs/compliance/ (EU AI Act, ISO 42001, NIST AI RMF), docs/guides/* not yet covered (hooks, modality handling, orchestration extensions, examples, OASF coverage, running workflows if gaps remain), templates/*.md and docs that render under /guides/skill-authoring/, composed skills' SKILL.md files that render under /guides/skills/ (e.g. digital-twin-sync-loop, debug-workflow, capability-gap-analysis, digital-twin-bootstrap, discover-capabilities, perspective-validation and its checklist), spec/RFC-0003 and docs/proposals/OASF_SAFETY_EXTENSIONS.md, docs/WHITEPAPER / spec/WHITEPAPER and GROUNDED_AGENCY where long runs remain, community docs (CONTRIBUTING, GOVERNANCE, PRESS_KIT, ROADMAP). Same rule: only where the text describes a structure, flow or sequence; only what the text says; accDescr: on every diagram; never invent. Checklists and procedures with ordered steps are sequences. Report each page's longest run before and after, and for pages still over 1,800px the section and the concrete reason. Then render a sample of 6 of the new diagrams as GitHub renders them: push is not allowed from here, so instead check every new block parses under the site renderer AND uses only syntax GitHub's Mermaid accepts (no unsupported front matter keys; the FAQ uses a title: front matter block - confirm by the scripts in ${SP}/pw/gh.mjs / gh2.mjs approach after integration, or note that the integrator must do it).` },\n  { name: 'audit', task: `Four fixes, each with a test that would fail without it:\n1. Sitewide: every rendered figure (each element the site marks as a figure with a \"Fig n\" number) must have a matching block in the page's Markdown copy (src/twins and dist/**/index.md). Today a test covers only the two Standard pages. Write a sitewide check (in check-dist or a test over dist) that counts figures per page against figure blocks in its Markdown copy, find every page where they differ, and fix each page so its copy carries the information (not just a heading).\n2. The model-deployment workflow figure legend (every workflow page) uses the same undo glyph for \"step rolls back\" and \"on failure, roll back\". Make each legend entry visually distinct, or merge them if they truly mean the same thing, and add a check that no legend on the site lists one glyph twice with different words.\n3. The layer-by-page heat map used on /start/, /spec/ and /background/ index pages: on /spec/ most cells are 0 and it shows how often each page names a capability as code. Judge whether it tells a reader something about those sections; where it does not, replace it with a figure drawn from those pages' own data (for /spec/: e.g. the documents and what each covers, versions, conformance fixtures counts, diagnostics per family) that a reader of that index would want. Keep run-length enforcement passing.\n4. Confirm the build-only glyph review sheet (dist/_review/glyphs/) never reaches the deployed site: read .github/workflows/site.yml and the build scripts; if it can be deployed, make the deploy step exclude it and add a check.` },\n]\n\nphase('Build')\nconst built = await parallel(BUILDERS.map((b) => () => agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are builder \"${b.name}\". Work in a new git worktree off the integration branch head: git -C ${WT} worktree add ../i125s-${b.name} -b feature/issue-125-s-${b.name} HEAD (path relative to ${WT}/..). Commit there in small commits; do not push. Save screenshots under ${SP}/visual/round3/${b.name}/.\\n\\nTask:\\n${b.task}\\n\\nReport: worktree path, branch, commits, what you built, check results with head SHA, screenshots opened, what you did not finish and why.`, { label: b.name, phase: 'Build' })))\n\nphase('Integrate')\nconst integ = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are the integrator. Builders' reports:\\n${built.map((r, i) => `--- ${BUILDERS[i].name} ---\\n${r}`).join('\\n')}\\n\\nIn ${WT} on feature/issue-125-site-visuals: merge feature/issue-125-s-prose-a, -s-prose-b, -s-audit with --no-ff in that order, resolve conflicts keeping both sides' intent. Run every check: npm ci, npm run check, npm test, npm run build, npm run check:axe (run-length enforcing), and from repo root pytest tests -q, python tools/validate_doc_links.py, python tools/sync_skill_schemas.py --check, python tools/validate_skill_refs.py. Fix anything failing. Push normally (never force). Then check that the new Mermaid diagrams render on GitHub: open at least 8 of them on github.com blob pages of the pushed branch with headless Chromium (adapt ${SP}/pw/gh.mjs or gh2.mjs), screenshot, OPEN the screenshots, and confirm each renders as a diagram, not an error box; include docs/FAQ.md which uses a title: front matter block. Fix any that fail on GitHub and push again. Remove the i125s-* worktrees after merging (keep branches). Report: merge commits, fixes, check results with the pushed SHA (git ls-remote), GitHub render results with screenshot paths, and the longest-run table for every page (node site/scripts/check-runs.mjs --all).`, { label: 'integrate', phase: 'Integrate' })\n\nphase('Review')\nconst review = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are an independent visual reviewer. You did not build any of this. Integrator's report:\\n${integ}\\n\\nBuild the pushed head of feature/issue-125-site-visuals in ${WT}/site (npm run build), serve dist on 127.0.0.1, and screenshot full pages at 1440 light, 1440 dark and 360 light with reduced motion. Judge each page against the maintainer's complaint (\"very text heavy, lacks iconography, better spacing and diagrams that convey the content better. It should still look good\"). Required pages, OPEN every full page image yourself: /capabilities/rollback/, /capabilities/synchronize/, /capabilities/audit/, /layers/remember/, /spec/1.0.0/, /spec/diagnostics/ (360 too), /spec/, /start/, /background/, /workflows/digital-twin-sync-loop/, /domains/multimodal/, /start/glossary/, /start/faq/, one compliance document, one composed-skill guide, /community/release-notes/. For each figure ask: does it convey content from the page's data, or does it only satisfy the run-length check? Does it repeat an adjacent table? Is anything cut, overlapping, under 12px, or scrolling sideways? Does its text match the source? Also count figures vs Markdown-copy figure blocks on 5 pages. Do not edit any repository file. Save shots under ${SP}/visual/round3/review/. Report findings ranked P1 (wrong/broken), P2 (clearly worse than it should be), P3 (polish), each with page, screenshot path, cause if known, and the change.`, { label: 'review', phase: 'Review' })\n\nphase('Fix')\nconst fixed = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou fix every finding in this review, in ${WT} on feature/issue-125-site-visuals, at the pushed head:\\n${review}\\n\\nFix P1, P2 and P3. A finding left unfixed needs a concrete reason (a maintainer decision, or data that does not exist). Keep run-length enforcement passing without new exemptions. Run every check listed in the integrator's step, push normally, and confirm local HEAD equals git ls-remote. Then take final screenshots of each page you changed and of: /capabilities/rollback/, /spec/, /start/glossary/, one compliance page, one composed-skill guide, at 1440 light, 1440 dark and 360 light, cropped to the part that shows the change; OPEN every one; save to ${SP}/visual/round3/final/. Report: each finding and what was done, check results with the pushed SHA, the longest-run table for every page, final screenshot paths.`, { label: 'fix', phase: 'Fix' })\n\nreturn { built, integ, review, fixed }\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
