{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"f112f2b77f3ab88fbe61a1af271a1736073843de833a59ddc4022a90e08db086","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"Before the next round I need your decision on four points. One is new: some of the files that got diagrams are read by agents as well as by people."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UZ52fNDJu7b9uNqVnZJ4V4","name":"AskUserQuestion","input":{"questions":[{"question":"Diagrams were added to files agents load as instructions, not only to documentation. This covers 6 composed skills' SKILL.md files, the perspective-validation checklist, and templates/composition_patterns.md and verification_patterns.md. Each template is also copied into about 25 skills' reference folders, which is about 1,300 added lines. Should these keep their diagrams?","header":"Skill files","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Remove from agent files (Recommended)","description":"Take the Mermaid blocks out of SKILL.md, CHECKLIST.md, templates/ and skills/*/reference/. The site draws its own figures for skill guide pages from the data instead. Agents' context stays as it was."},{"label":"Keep them","description":"Diagrams stay in the skill and template files and their copies. GitHub shows them, and agents read them as text (each has a one-line description)."},{"label":"Keep in SKILL.md only","description":"Remove from templates and their reference copies (the duplication), keep in the composed skills' own SKILL.md and the checklist."}]},{"question":"CHANGELOG.md got 18 diagrams on entries that describe a flow or structure. Each version's section becomes its GitHub Release notes, so the diagrams would show up in releases too. Keep them?","header":"Changelog","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Remove from CHANGELOG (Recommended)","description":"Release notes stay short text. The release-notes page on the site can still draw its own data figure (changes per release)."},{"label":"Keep them","description":"GitHub Releases render Mermaid, so the diagrams appear in future release notes too."}]},{"question":"The design spec says capability examples and workflow step cards start with the first one open. The builders made them all start closed, so the page is shorter and less text-heavy, and the full text is one click away. Which do you want?","header":"Folded text","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"First one open (Recommended)","description":"Follow the spec: the first example and the first step card are open, so a reader sees one full instance without clicking."},{"label":"All closed","description":"Keep the shorter pages; every example and step card starts closed."}]},{"question":"Some figures departed from the spec. Six Standard figures are drawn more simply than specified: the run replay, the timeout, the quorum and the trust cascade (F-S14, F-S24b, F-S27, F-S28), the layer model layout (F-S1), and the keyword pips, which are ink-coloured (F-S6). The spec also gives status glyphs only to RFC statuses, but every status in use now has one. Which do you want?","header":"Spec gaps","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Build to the spec (Recommended)","description":"Redraw the six Standard figures as specified. Keep a status glyph for every status in use, and finish the draft glyphs, since you chose one icon per concept."},{"label":"Keep as built","description":"Accept the simpler figures and the extra status glyphs as they are."}]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
