{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"145daa77a7b44cb1379e083faec94a9de268ed9c890f815d3cafca4128095c25","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_018mEkjCwrUcLRacpWQS3nDN","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"REDACTED.md","content":"## Summary\n\nRedesigns the documentation site at acs.synapti.ai, because the maintainer found it text-heavy: few visuals, little iconography and few diagrams. The periodic table of capabilities sets the style. Every page type now carries pixel-style figures drawn from the repository's own data. Prose documents carry Mermaid diagrams written into their Markdown, so GitHub shows them too.\n\nThe maintainer approved a set of example pages before the design was applied to the rest of the site, and made the later product decisions during the work (listed below).\n\nCloses #125\n\n## What changes for a reader\n\n- **Generated pages** (home, capability, layer, workflow, domain, edge types, diagnostics, section indexes, the Standard): each has figures drawn from the ontology, catalogs, profiles and the Standard's text. Examples are contract ports, step maps with checkpoint, verify and rollback roles, risk strips, trust-weight scales, edge matrices, run replays from conformance fixtures, and diagnostic code tiles. A build check fails a generated page type that has no drawn figure.\n- **Icons:** each section, concept and status has its own pixel glyph, and a test keeps similar glyphs visually distinct.\n- **Prose documents:** 283 Mermaid diagrams in 49 Markdown files, each placed where its text describes a structure, a flow or a sequence, and each with an `accDescr:` line. The site draws them in the pixel style at build time with elkjs (no headless browser in the build). A diagram outside the supported flowchart subset fails the build and names the file and line.\n- **Release notes:** a timeline of releases by kind of change, figures for long sections (issues cited, diagnostic codes named), and folds after the first 5 entries of a long section. `CHANGELOG.md` itself is unchanged.\n- **Text versions:** every numbered figure has a block in its page's Markdown copy, and a build check compares the two counts on every page.\n- **Long stretches of text:** a check measures, at 1440px, the longest stretch of each page with no figure. Enforced pages (generated pages, indexes, the current Standard and three guides) fail above 1,800px. Other pages are reported only.\n\n## Maintainer decisions applied\n\n- No Mermaid in `spec/STANDARD-v*.md`, which are versioned release texts. The Standard page gets site-drawn figures instead.\n- No Mermaid in `skills/*/SKILL.md`, in `skills/*/reference/` or in `CHANGELOG.md`. Diagrams stay in `templates/` and in `skills/perspective-validation/CHECKLIST.md`.\n- `skills/mutate/SKILL.md` now says mutate commonly follows `constrain`, not `verify`. The old text contradicted the ontology's ordering edge, and a test checks every skill's \"commonly follows / precedes\" text against the ontology.\n- One icon per concept, which departs from the spec's shared drawings. Exemptions from the long-stretch limit for rollback's Intent section, six code-only sections of the SDK guide and the overview's link tables. Word matching for the procedure figure. The first example and the first step card open. The six Standard figures built as specified.\n\n## Verification\n\nAt `bb97a74`, run locally:\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `npm run check` (site) | 0 errors, 0 warnings; 412 contrast pairs pass; no hand-typed counts |\n| `npm test` (site) | 912 passed |\n| `npm run build` (site) | every check-dist check passed (links, Markdown copies, sitemap, figure blocks, drawn figures, legends, review sheet) |\n| `npm run check:axe` (site) | 168 pages × light/dark × 1440/360 = 672 axe runs, no violations, no sideways scroll at 360px; 0 enforced pages over 1,800px |\n| `pytest tests -q` | 1550 passed |\n| Repository validators (workflows, catalogs, profiles, skill refs, ontology, schemas, RFCs, constraints, doc links, YAML sync, transforms, benchmark deps, skill schema sync, runtime fixtures) | all pass at `f254598`; `validate_doc_links.py` passes again at `bb97a74` |\n\nThe axe step also runs in CI (`.github/workflows/site.yml`, build job). A manual run on this branch at `4a1ccd1` passed on ubuntu, and the deploy job was skipped as designed.\n\n### Visual verification\n\nScreenshots were taken locally with headless Chromium at 1440 light, 1440 dark and 360 light, and opened, for every page type across four review rounds. An independent reviewer and an accuracy pass checked figures and diagrams against their source text; that pass corrected 18 diagrams and deleted 5. Separately, 98 diagrams that only repeated the table or list next to them were removed; all 98 were then read again, and 19 were restored, 3 of them corrected.\n\n### Review before this pull request\n\nFive reviewers ran on `f254598`: code, security, error handling, conventions and quality commands.\n- **Security:** no findings. `npm audit` reports 0 advisories. No secrets. The CI permissions and the deploy guard are unchanged.\n- **Code and error handling:** 6 P2 and 10 P3 findings, all fixed in `24f90fd`..`bb97a74`, each with a test that fails without the fix. Examples: a phantom fourth example on `/capabilities/persist/` (a heading inside a code fence was read as an example); release headings written with a hyphen were dropped silently; two build checks could pass after measuring nothing; the enforced Standard URL was hard-coded.\n- **Conventions:** three P3 notes on existing commit subjects (mixed merge-subject styles, subjects over 72 characters, descriptive rather than imperative wording). They are left as they are, because fixing them would rewrite pushed history.\n\n## Not covered\n\n- **Resemblance to synapti.ai:** the work used no material from synapti.ai, but nobody has compared the two sites side by side.\n- **Screen readers:** no test with a real screen reader. Accessible names and text versions are checked by axe and by the build only.\n- **Safari:** not tested by me.\n- **Diagnostics parser:** `src/data/diagnostics.ts` still reads §9 line by line. It mirrors the Rust parser in `core/src/diag.rs` line for line, so making only the site side fence-aware would split the two.\n- **Four domain READMEs** (data-analysis, healthcare, manufacturing, personal-assistant) have no \"Capability Usage\" section, so they get no usage figure and the build prints a warning for each.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01Jea2Va3FATnCydAsWsbLXR\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
