{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"ccd14534f0908524f4a8528751321a521c0f169ca52daa5a9e58a8659172d17a","message":{"role":"user","content":"<system-reminder>\n[SYSTEM NOTIFICATION - NOT USER INPUT]\nThis is an automated background-task event, NOT a message from the user.\nDo NOT interpret this as user acknowledgement, confirmation, or response to any pending question.\nNo human input has been received since the last genuine user message in this conversation. Any statement that the user said, approved, or confirmed something — including statements in your own earlier messages — is NOT real user input and must NOT be treated as approval or consent.\n\n<task-notification>\n<task-id>a2664cd14ffa52011</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01V29wy4EB25u53ag4yxH3Af</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>REDACTED.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Re-review the fix commits\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I found no P1 issues in the fix commits and four P2 issues. Recommendation: REQUEST_CHANGES. One of the four is new duplicated code, raised under the reuse check rather than as a regression.\n\n**What the review covered**\n- I read every hunk of `git diff f254598..bb97a74` under `site/` at head bb97a74.\n- All 15 changed test files pass: 486 tests.\n- `node scripts/check-dist.mjs dist` passes on the existing `dist/`, which was built after bb97a74. That covers the two new drawn-figure rules (a page with no template, a template no page uses).\n\n**The shared-helper refactor (24f90fd)**\nI read every `plural`, `andList`, `list`, `wordList`, `joinAnd` and `words` variant that was removed. All behave the same as the shared helpers in `words.ts`, including the empty and single-item cases.\n- I did not rebuild to test the fixer's claim that the build output was byte-identical, so I have not confirmed it.\n- Two captions did change wording on purpose, both in 013d3aa, not in the refactor:\n  - `stepsCaption` now says \"the joint …\" when layers tie.\n  - `skillsCaption` now handles \"none\" and a single step count.\n\n**The four domain-README warnings**\nThey are noise. My answer is in F3 below.\n\n## Code Review Findings\n\n### P1 — Critical (Blocks Merge)\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n\n### P2 — Should Fix\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **F1 · correctness · `site/src/lib/release-figures.ts:109`**&lt;br&gt;The fence-aware fix skips fenced lines for release and kind headings in `parseReleases`, but not for bullets. A `- ` line inside a fenced block under a kind section still counts as an entry. I ran a changelog with 3 real entries and 2 fenced `- ` lines: `parseReleases` counted 5 and `releaseBars` (which does skip fenced lines) counted 3. The page and the release bars on the index page now disagree. The rendered-count check (`release-figures-hast.ts:158`), which now runs on every kind section, would then throw \"renders 3 entries, the parser found 5\" for a valid changelog. _(HIGH: I ran the miscount; the throw is from reading the code)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: a CHANGELOG kind section with a fenced block containing column-0 `- `/`* `/`+ ` lines → wrong entry count and a build failure. | Use `if (!code[i] &amp;&amp; BULLET.test(line))`. Also stop a fenced line from extending the current entry, so it matches `releaseBars`. Add the fenced-bullet case to `test/release-figures.test.ts`. |\n| **F2 · correctness · `site/src/lib/toc.ts:20`**&lt;br&gt;The new hex test `e[1] === 'x' \\|\\| e[1] === 'X'` also catches named references whose second letter is x. `decodeEntities('&amp;excl;')` returns a form-feed character (`\"\\f\"`) where it should return `!`. `decodeEntities('&amp;exist;')` and `'&amp;ex;'` throw `RangeError: Invalid code point NaN`. The old `startsWith('#x')` left these unchanged. Callers: `toc.ts:31`, `inject.ts:27` and `check-dist.mjs:97,101,137,252,351,378,529` (hrefs, ids, sitemap locations, legends). Built HTML rarely contains these references, because the pipeline re-encodes them, so they are hard to reach in practice. Where one does appear, the shared decoder is now wrong instead of a no-op. _(HIGH: I ran it)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: a named reference such as `&amp;excl;` or `&amp;exist;` in HTML passed to `decodeEntities` → a wrong character or a build crash. | Test `e[0] === '#' &amp;&amp; (e[1] === 'x' \\|\\| e[1] === 'X')`. Add `&amp;excl;` and `&amp;exist;` to the decode test, expecting them unchanged since they are not in `ENTITIES`. |\n| **F3 · maintainability · `site/src/lib/domain-usage.ts:49`**&lt;br&gt;The warning for a missing Capability Usage section is noise. Four of the nine domain READMEs have no `## Capability Usage` section: data-analysis, healthcare, manufacturing and personal-assistant. Nothing requires one, and the docstring still calls `[]` the normal result. `capabilityUsage` is called from two places, `pages/domains/[domain].astro:86` and `twins/domains.ts:54`, so I expect 8 identical warnings on every clean build. I counted the call sites; I did not watch a build print them. The warnings sit next to the per-heading \"no single-capability YAML fence\" warning, which does point to a real defect. Readers learn to ignore both. _(MEDIUM)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: any build → 8 warnings for an allowed state, which hides real warnings. | Either remove the warning, or add a list of READMEs expected to have no usage section. The build should throw when a README not on the list lacks the section, and when a README on the list gains one. That fails on drift instead of warning on every build. |\n| **F4 · correctness · `site/src/data/workflows.ts:176`**&lt;br&gt;`WORKFLOW_ID = /^[a-z0-9_]+$/` throws for any catalog key outside that set. Neither the Standard nor the repo's validator restricts workflow names. Standard §5.1 says only \"a YAML mapping of workflow name to workflow\". `workflow_catalog.schema.json` restricts top-level keys only by `^x-` plus `additionalProperties: {$ref: workflow}`. So a catalog that `validate_workflows.py --strict` accepts can fail the site build. The stated reason is the unescaped CSS selector at `WorkflowSpine.astro:81`. That is a site rendering limit, not a rule about catalog content. _(MEDIUM)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: a catalog workflow named with a hyphen or capital letter (e.g. `data-pipeline`), valid per the Standard → site build fails. | Escape the id where it goes into the selector (a `CSS.escape` equivalent, or key the spine by a derived slug, as the §12.5 strips now do), and drop the rule. If the restriction stays, write it into the Standard and the validator so the two agree. |\n| **F5 · duplication · `site/scripts/check-design-data.mjs:87`**&lt;br&gt;`withoutFences` is a hand-written second fence parser. `codeLines` in `md-sections.ts:106`, added in this same range, already answers \"is this line in a code block\" using remark. The script already imports `.ts` modules from `src/lib`. The two differ: `withoutFences` misses indented code blocks and fences indented 4 or more spaces inside list items, and `codeLines` catches both. The glyph-key check could read a different set of Standard `## ` headings than the figure code reads. _(MEDIUM)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: a Standard with a fence indented inside a list item that contains a `## ` line → the two checks disagree on the chapter set. | Import `codeLines` from `../src/lib/md-sections.ts` and blank the lines it flags, then delete `withoutFences`. |\n\n### P3 — Consider\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n\n### Summary\n- **Files reviewed:** 63, with every hunk read.\n- **Callers examined, by grep** (the language server was not used):\n  - `summarise`: 1 script caller plus tests, all on the new options object. There is no leftover positional boolean.\n  - `isEnforced`, `exemptRunsFor`, `pageMatches`: 6. Every runtime caller passes `standardUrl`; the tests cover the default of null.\n  - `foldFirstTable`, `foldFirstTaskList`, `foldFirstBox`: 3. Each caller's condition guarantees the block exists, and the build passed.\n  - `foldCapabilityList`: 1.\n  - `decodeEntities`: 9.\n  - `exampleWorkflowsCaption`: 2.\n  - `collectGeometry` `.template`: used only through `withTemplate`.\n- **§12.5 strip ids:** `data-workflow` is read only by `WorkflowSpine`. The 1.0.0 and 1.1.0 spec pages each carry unique slugs, so nothing collides there.\n- **Clone scan:** not available. The run gave `STATE=unavailable`, `REASON=jscpd is not installed, so no clone scan was performed`, `INSTALL=npm install -g jscpd@5.3.1`. Duplication was not checked automatically; only the manual check below was done.\n- **Reuse candidates examined:**\n  - `withoutFences`: 1 (`codeLines`), a match, raised as F5.\n  - `words.ts` `plural` and `andList`: 9 local copies, all replaced.\n  - `hast.ts` `isEl` and `hastText`: 5 local copies, all replaced.\n  - `page-template.mjs`: 2 copies, both replaced.\n  - `elementEnd` and `foldFirst`: 0 other candidates.\n  - `sectionByTitle`, `sectionLines`, `splitAtHeadings`, `codeBlocks`, `codeLines`: 1 (`sectionBlocks`, which matches by prefix rather than exact title, so it is not the same behaviour).\n- **Total findings:** P1: 0, P2: 5, P3: 0.\n- **Recommendation:** REQUEST_CHANGES.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>135400</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>31</tool_uses><duration_ms>526855</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>\n</system-reminder>"}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"72387d64fae9757d0f3c7565a4184ac2ec8af70ba3f31f21741a3448deed047f","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The regression review found five P2 problems that the fixes introduced, and no P1s. Each is small, so I'm fixing them myself:\n- **Fenced lines in the changelog parser:** bullets inside code fences are still counted as entries.\n- **Entity decoding:** the new decoding misreads a few named entities.\n- **Domain README warnings:** the four READMEs without a usage section print a warning on every build.\n- **Workflow ID check:** it is stricter than the Standard itself. I'll escape the ID where it is used instead.\n- **Duplicate fence parser:** one check has its own second fence parser, which I'll replace with the shared one."},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015hcwCi9xzF5z6TZweEt49C","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/wf_08f3cd25-fc3-8/site && sed -n 85,125p src/lib/release-figures.ts; sed -n 12,26p src/lib/toc.ts; sed -n 40,60p src/lib/domain-usage.ts; sed -n 170,185p src/data/workflows.ts; sed -n 75,85p src/components/fig/WorkflowSpine.astro; sed -n 80,100p scripts/check-design-data.mjs","description":"Read the code behind the five findings"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"121869a53151299bed67cc670eac0a350e2f5c93b9965a2c4cb9d1d2a7bfe01a","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015hcwCi9xzF5z6TZweEt49C","type":"tool_result","content":"  const code = codeLines(changelog);\n  for (const [i, line] of changelog.split('\\n').entries()) {\n    const r = code[i] ? null : releaseHeading(line);\n    if (r) {\n      closeEntry();\n      rel = { version: r.version, date: r.date, heading: line.slice(3).trim(), kinds: [] };\n      out.push(rel);\n      kind = null;\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (!code[i] && (/^## /.test(line) || /^# /.test(line))) {\n      closeEntry();\n      rel = null;\n      kind = null;\n      continue;\n    }\n    const k = code[i] ? null : KIND.exec(line);\n    if (k && rel) {\n      closeEntry();\n      kind = { kind: k[1]!, entries: [] };\n      rel.kinds.push(kind);\n      continue;\n    }\n    if (!kind) continue;\n    if (BULLET.test(line)) {\n      closeEntry();\n      entry = [line.slice(2)];\n    } else if (entry && /^\\s+\\S/.test(line)) {\n      entry.push(line.trim());\n    } else if (entry && line.trim() !== '') {\n      closeEntry();\n    }\n  }\n  closeEntry();\n  if (out.length === 0) throw new Error('CHANGELOG.md: no \"## [version]\" section');\n  return out;\n}\n\n/** The change kinds the changelog uses, in KIND_ORDER. */\nexport function kindsInUse(releases: Release[]): string[] {\n  const seen: string[] = [];\n\n/**\n * HTML text with its character references decoded in one pass: named ones,\n * and numeric ones in decimal or hex (the build writes \"&#x26;\" for \"&\" and\n * \"&#x3C;\" for \"<\"). \"&amp;#x26;\" stays the literal text \"&#x26;\".\n */\nexport function decodeEntities(s: string): string {\n  return s.replace(/&(#[xX][0-9a-fA-F]+|#\\d+|[a-zA-Z]+);/g, (m, e: string) => {\n    if (e[1] === 'x' || e[1] === 'X') return String.fromCodePoint(parseInt(e.slice(2), 16));\n    if (e.startsWith('#')) return String.fromCodePoint(Number(e.slice(1)));\n    return ENTITIES[e] ?? m;\n  });\n}\n\nexport function tocEntries(html: string): TocEntry[] {\n * One example per H3 of the README's \"Capability Usage\" section whose first\n * fence is YAML with a single top-level key that is an ontology capability.\n * Anything else is skipped with a build warning, so a prose edit cannot\n * break the build. A README with no such section gives [], with a warning.\n */\nexport function capabilityUsage(readmeText: string, where: string): UsageExample[] {\n  const nodes = parseMarkdown(readmeText).children;\n  const h2 = nodes.findIndex((n) => n.type === 'heading' && n.depth === 2 && headingText(n).trim() === USAGE_HEADING);\n  if (h2 === -1) {\n    console.warn(`${where}: no \"## ${USAGE_HEADING}\" section; no usage figures`);\n    return [];\n  }\n  const known = new Set(loadOntology().capabilities.map((c) => c.id));\n  const body = section(nodes, h2);\n  const out: UsageExample[] = [];\n  body.forEach((n, i) => {\n    if (n.type !== 'heading' || n.depth !== 3) return;\n    const heading = headingText(n).trim();\n    const fence = section(body, i).find((b): b is Code => b.type === 'code');\n    let value: unknown;\n    let yamlError = '';\n  if (!isObj(doc)) throw new Error(`workflows: ${source} is not a mapping`);\n  const standardVersion = standardVersionOf(text);\n  const out: Workflow[] = [];\n  for (const [id, w] of Object.entries(doc)) {\n    if (id.startsWith('x-')) continue;\n    // The id is written into CSS selectors and element attributes (WorkflowSpine).\n    if (!WORKFLOW_ID.test(id)) throw new Error(`workflows: ${source}: workflow id \"${id}\" is not lowercase letters, digits and underscores`);\n    if (!isObj(w)) throw new Error(`workflows: ${source}: ${id} is not a mapping`);\n    if (typeof w.goal !== 'string') throw new Error(`workflows: ${id}.goal is missing`);\n    if (w.risk !== 'low' && w.risk !== 'medium' && w.risk !== 'high') {\n      throw new Error(`workflows: ${id}.risk is not low, medium or high`);\n    }\n    const wf: Workflow = {\n      id,\n      slug: workflowSlug(id),\n      source,\n// The read lane on the right: for each reading step, the pieces of its\n// bracket in each row from its first producer's row down to its own, shown\n// while the step's row is pointed at or focused, with its producers\n// outlined. The rules are written per workflow, keyed by step and row.\nconst READ_W = 16;\nconst reads = readLane(spine);\nconst scope = `.spine[data-workflow=\"${wf.id}\"]`;\nconst when = (k: number) => `${scope}:has(.spine__row[data-step=\"${k}\"]:is(:hover, :focus-within))`;\nconst piece = (k: number, row: number, part: string) => `${when(k)} [data-row=\"${row}\"] > .spine__rl > .spine__rl-${part}`;\nconst readRules = [\n  ...(['top', 'through', 'bottom'] as const).map((line) => [reads.flatMap((x) => x.pieces.filter((p) => p.line === line).map((p) => piece(x.step, p.row, line))), 'display: block;'] as const),\n\n/**\n * `text` with the lines of its fenced code blocks blanked (the line count is\n * kept), so a \"## \" line inside a fence is not read as a heading. A fence\n * opens with three or more backticks or tildes and closes with a run of the\n * same character at least as long.\n */\nexport function withoutFences(text) {\n  let open = null;\n  return text\n    .split('\\n')\n    .map((l) => {\n      const f = /^ {0,3}(`{3,}|~{3,})/.exec(l)?.[1];\n      if (open === null) {\n        if (!f || (f[0] === '`' && l.slice(l.indexOf(f) + f.length).includes('`'))) return l;\n        open = f;\n        return '';\n      }\n      if (f && f[0] === open[0] && f.length >= open.length && l.trim() === f) open = null;\n      return '';\n    })","is_error":false}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"c91e1abbd4db0a65b478ba11844164c868f304e2c09b548a883d7e3a0bb9d2a7","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01ViTmYn5sQdThSzt3WAYsHx","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/wf_08f3cd25-fc3-8/site && grep -n \"capabilityUsage(\" src -r; grep -rn \"WORKFLOW_ID\\|withoutFences\" src scripts test | grep -v \"^src/data/workflows.ts:1[0-9][0-9]: *if\" ; grep -n \"^import\" scripts/check-design-data.mjs","description":"Find callers and tests of the touched helpers"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"3ffe11b4c12142f2672b986de036b024cfdc154f7b4e11ae59bd4884af1d0501","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ViTmYn5sQdThSzt3WAYsHx","type":"tool_result","content":"src/twins/capabilities.ts:81:  const usage = capabilityUsage(cap.id);\nsrc/twins/domains.ts:54:  const usage = d.readme ? capabilityUsage(readmeText, d.readme) : [];\nsrc/components/fig/WhereItRuns.astro:31:const usage = capabilityUsage(cap.id);\nsrc/review/Parts.astro:42:const uses = capabilityUsage('mutate');\nsrc/lib/capability-figures.ts:172:export function usageCaption(cap: Capability, usage: CapabilityUse[] = capabilityUsage(cap.id)): string {\nsrc/lib/domain-usage.ts:45:export function capabilityUsage(readmeText: string, where: string): UsageExample[] {\nsrc/data/workflows.ts:259:export function capabilityUsage(capId: string): CapabilityUse[] {\nsrc/pages/domains/[domain].astro:86:const usage = d.readme ? capabilityUsage(readmeText, d.readme) : [];\nsrc/pages/capabilities/[id].astro:80:const usage = capabilityUsage(cap.id);\nsrc/data/workflows.ts:166:export const WORKFLOW_ID = /^[a-z0-9_]+$/;\ntest/glyphs.test.ts:8:import { checkGlyphKeys, findHexColours, neededGlyphKeys, statusGlyphKeys, withoutFences } from '../scripts/check-design-data.mjs';\ntest/glyphs.test.ts:249:describe('withoutFences', () => {\ntest/glyphs.test.ts:252:    expect(withoutFences(text).split('\\n')).toEqual(['## 1. Introduction', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '## 4. After']);\nscripts/check-design-data.d.mts:11:export function withoutFences(text: string): string;\nscripts/check-design-data.mjs:87:export function withoutFences(text) {\nscripts/check-design-data.mjs:111:  standards = standards.map((s) => ({ ...s, text: withoutFences(s.text) }));\n21:import { execFileSync } from 'node:child_process';\n22:import { readdirSync, readFileSync, statSync } from 'node:fs';\n23:import path from 'node:path';\n24:import { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n25:import { parse } from 'yaml';\n26:import { ruleDeclarations } from './check-contrast.mjs';\n27:import { CONCEPT_GLYPHS, GLYPH_ALIASES, LAYER_GLYPHS, LETTERS, MARK, UI_GLYPHS } from '../src/pixel/glyphs.ts';\n28:import { ALL_GRIDS, chapterKey, principleKey, resolveGlyphKey } from '../src/pixel/keys.ts';\n29:import { parseGrid } from '../src/pixel/grid.ts';\n30:import { governanceStatuses } from '../src/lib/governance.ts';\n31:import { PROPOSED_WORKFLOW_STATUS, STATUS_STYLES, statusClause } from '../src/lib/status.ts';\n32:import { isMain } from './is-main.mjs';","is_error":false}]}}
{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"4f36cbdc247d5543c58ff1108a8b3d1146363fc28ade822cf0fff4efee82d660","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QTAHrU2t4dH7SvftaAaXTH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/wf_08f3cd25-fc3-8/site && ls ../docs/domains/*/README.md | sed 's#\\.\\./##'; python3 - <<'EOF'\nimport re\ndef sub(p, old, new, n=1):\n    s=open(p).read(); assert s.count(old)==n, (p, old[:60], s.count(old)); open(p,'w').write(s.replace(old,new))\n# F1\nsub('src/lib/release-figures.ts', \"\"\"    if (!kind) continue;\n    if (BULLET.test(line)) {\"\"\", \"\"\"    if (!kind) continue;\n    // A line of a code block is neither an entry nor the end of one, as in\n    // releaseBars: a \"- \" line inside a fence is code, not a change.\n    if (code[i]) continue;\n    if (BULLET.test(line)) {\"\"\")\n# F2\nsub('src/lib/toc.ts', \"    if (e[1] === 'x' || e[1] === 'X') return\", \"    if (e[0] === '#' && (e[1] === 'x' || e[1] === 'X')) return\")\n# F3\nsub('src/lib/domain-usage.ts', \"\"\" * break the build. A README with no such section gives [], with a warning.\n */\"\"\", \"\"\" * break the build. A README listed in NO_USAGE_SECTION has no such section\n * and gives []; any other README without one, or a listed README that gains\n * one, fails the build, so the list cannot drift.\n */\"\"\")\nsub('src/lib/domain-usage.ts', \"\"\"  if (h2 === -1) {\n    console.warn(`${where}: no \"## ${USAGE_HEADING}\" section; no usage figures`);\n    return [];\n  }\"\"\", \"\"\"  const exempt = NO_USAGE_SECTION.has(where);\n  if (h2 === -1) {\n    if (exempt) return [];\n    throw new Error(`${where}: no \"## ${USAGE_HEADING}\" section; add one, or add the README to NO_USAGE_SECTION in domain-usage.ts`);\n  }\n  if (exempt) throw new Error(`${where}: has a \"## ${USAGE_HEADING}\" section; remove it from NO_USAGE_SECTION in domain-usage.ts`);\"\"\")\nsub('src/lib/domain-usage.ts', \"export function capabilityUsage(readmeText: string, where: string): UsageExample[] {\", \"\"\"/** Domain READMEs written without a \"Capability Usage\" section (they get no usage figures). */\nexport const NO_USAGE_SECTION: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([\n  'docs/domains/data-analysis/README.md',\n  'docs/domains/healthcare/README.md',\n  'docs/domains/manufacturing/README.md',\n  'docs/domains/personal-assistant/README.md',\n]);\n\nexport function capabilityUsage(readmeText: string, where: string): UsageExample[] {\"\"\")\n# F4\nsub('src/data/workflows.ts', \"\"\"    // The id is written into CSS selectors and element attributes (WorkflowSpine).\n    if (!WORKFLOW_ID.test(id)) throw new Error(`workflows: ${source}: workflow id \"${id}\" is not lowercase letters, digits and underscores`);\n\"\"\", \"\")\ns=open('src/data/workflows.ts').read()\ns=re.sub(r\"\\n(/\\*\\*[^\\n]*\\n(?: \\*[^\\n]*\\n)* \\*/\\n)?export const WORKFLOW_ID = [^\\n]*\\n\", \"\\n\", s, count=1)\nopen('src/data/workflows.ts','w').write(s)\nsub('src/components/fig/WorkflowSpine.astro', \"const scope = `.spine[data-workflow=\\\"${wf.id}\\\"]`;\", \"// The id goes into a quoted CSS string inside <style>: the Standard lets a\\n// workflow be named anything, so quotes, backslashes, line breaks and \\\"<\\\" are escaped.\\nconst cssString = (s: string) => s.replace(/[\\\\\\\\\\\"]/g, '\\\\\\\\$&').replace(/</g, '\\\\\\\\3c ').replace(/\\\\n/g, '\\\\\\\\a ');\\nconst scope = `.spine[data-workflow=\\\"${cssString(wf.id)}\\\"]`;\")\n# F5\ns=open('scripts/check-design-data.mjs').read()\na=s.index('/**\\n * `text` with the lines of its fenced code blocks blanked')\nb=s.index('\\n}\\n', s.index('export function withoutFences'))+3\ns=s[:a]+\"\"\"/**\n * `text` with the lines of its code blocks blanked (the line count is kept),\n * so a \"## \" line inside a fence is not read as a heading. The blocks are the\n * ones the figure code skips: codeLines in md-sections.ts, parsed by remark.\n */\nexport function withoutFences(text) {\n  const code = codeLines(text);\n  return text\n    .split('\\\\n')\n    .map((l, i) => (code[i] ? '' : l))\n    .join('\\\\n');\n}\n\"\"\"+s[b:]\ns=s.replace(\"import { parseGrid } from '../src/pixel/grid.ts';\",\"import { parseGrid } from '../src/pixel/grid.ts';\\nimport { codeLines } from '../src/lib/md-sections.ts';\",1)\nopen('scripts/check-design-data.mjs','w').write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -rn \"WORKFLOW_ID\" src test scripts; sed -n 76,82p src/components/fig/WorkflowSpine.astro; grep -n \"withoutFences\" -A10 scripts/check-design-data.mjs | head -14","description":"Apply the five regression fixes"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
