{"type":"api-request-blob","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","hash":"503f4a45fe48c444feb8946bfe63edda5dca56d07cf3097f04d364e8ee483f06","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The score page gives only totals, 24/100, with the weakest areas being discovery (5/20) and usability (4/40) and no per-check detail. I'm reading ora.ai's methodology to see what each check looks for.\n\n","signature":""},{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01VRRDciExBoBM7PWCeuoqwu","name":"SendMessage","input":{"to":"acb3da630f9668b63","summary":"Fetch ora.ai methodology and docs check definitions","message":"Now fetch https://ora.ai/methodology and https://ora.ai/llms.txt (and any page they link that defines individual checks, e.g. /docs pages about checks, scoring or the ax audit CLI). Report every individual check ora.ai runs, grouped by goal/category: its exact name, what it looks for (URLs, files, headers, formats, e.g. /llms.txt, /llms-full.txt, /.well-known/* files, robots.txt directives, sitemap, markdown alternates via Accept header or .md URLs, Link headers, JSON-LD types, agent skills files, MCP server cards, OpenAPI, NLWeb, auth.md), and how it is scored. Quote names exactly and include URLs of the pages you used. Keep it complete; this is the spec I will implement against."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}]}}
