{"type":"bridge-session","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","bridgeSessionId":"cse_01Jea2Va3FATnCydAsWsbLXR","lastSequenceNum":0,"ownerAccountUuid":"8eea3ac3-49e3-4204-8c16-e0957a2b6be7","ownerOrganizationUuid":"847d2ebf-3377-472a-b66f-421105089bbf"}
{"type":"file-history-delta","messageId":"4d9d35fb-e614-4b31-9cd5-528fa07c4007","snapshotMessageId":"fc9e9837-3d70-4f44-ab26-94f7085de885","trackingPath":"REDACTED.md","backup":{"backupFileName":null,"version":1,"backupTime":"2026-11-13T13:23:28.644Z","realParentDir":"REDACTED"},"timestamp":"2026-11-13T13:23:28.645Z"}
{"parentUuid":"e97d7ebb-5f96-4758-922f-1fc0d4662116","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgzzvAa9S9i2ndUmv4Kwh","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_012aSsYk9VFX98X2FZyPfVTF","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"REDACTED.md","content":"## Context\n\nThe documentation site at acs.synapti.ai was launched under #120 and redesigned under #125. An agent-readiness scan by ora.ai (scoring version 3, scanned 2026-11-13) rates it 24/100, grade F: discovery 5/20, access 13/30, usability 4/40. Its summary is that agents can reach the site but find no API, no MCP server and no developer docs to integrate with. The standard is written for AI agents, so agents are a primary audience for the site.\n\n## Current State\n\n- Agents can read every page as Markdown at `<page>/index.md`, and `/llms.txt` and `/llms-full.txt` list the pages. `/llms.txt` is longer than the length agents are expected to read.\n- Nothing describes the site to agents in a machine-readable way: no structured data about the project, no discovery files, no catalog of the skills the repository ships, and no dates on sitemap entries.\n- The standard's data (capabilities, edges, workflows, profiles, diagnostic codes) is available only as YAML on GitHub or as prose on the pages. There is no API and no description of one.\n- An agent cannot ask the site for Markdown at a page's own URL, a missing page answers only in HTML, and appending `.md` to a page URL returns 404.\n- There is no MCP server for the standard, and browser agents have no in-page tools.\n- There are no about, contact or privacy pages.\n\n## Objective\n\nAn agent can find the site, learn what the standard is and when to use it, read any page or data item in a machine format, and query the standard through a documented API and an MCP server, without scraping HTML. Readers' experience of the site does not get worse.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An agent can discover, from the site's own well-known locations and response headers, where the API, its description, the MCP server, the skills and the Markdown copies are.\n- [ ] The standard's capabilities, edges, workflows, domain profiles and diagnostic codes are served read-only in JSON, with a published OpenAPI description whose operations each have an id, a description and typed responses.\n- [ ] A read-only MCP server for the standard answers over the current transport, lists described tools with typed inputs and outputs, marks them read-only, and returns structured errors for bad calls.\n- [ ] A page requested with `Accept: text/markdown`, or at its URL with `.md` appended, is answered with its Markdown copy; HTML requests still get HTML. A missing page answers 404 with a short Markdown pointer to the docs when Markdown is acceptable.\n- [ ] `/llms.txt` stays within the length agents read, says when to use the standard, and links per-section files.\n- [ ] The home page carries structured data about the project and its organization, and complete social metadata; the sitemap carries last-modified dates.\n- [ ] The repository's skills are listed in an agent-skills index with a digest per skill.\n- [ ] Browser agents get read-only in-page tools for searching and reading the docs, where the browser supports them.\n- [ ] About, contact and privacy pages exist; contact goes through GitHub, and the privacy page states what the site does and does not collect.\n- [ ] Every existing site check still passes (build checks, accessibility in both themes at 1440 and 360 pixels, contrast), and a new ora.ai scan after deploy scores higher, with the remaining gaps listed.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3853,"cache_read_input_tokens":558087,"output_tokens":1480,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3853,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":1480,"cache_read_input_tokens":558087,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3853,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3853},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":12540,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_012aSsYk9VFX98X2FZyPfVTF":{"file_path":"REDACTED.md","content":"## Context\n\nThe documentation site at acs.synapti.ai was launched under #120 and redesigned under #125. An agent-readiness scan by ora.ai (scoring version 3, scanned 2026-11-13) rates it 24/100, grade F: discovery 5/20, access 13/30, usability 4/40. Its summary is that agents can reach the site but find no API, no MCP server and no developer docs to integrate with. The standard is written for AI agents, so agents are a primary audience for the site.\n\n## Current State\n\n- Agents can read every page as Markdown at `<page>/index.md`, and `/llms.txt` and `/llms-full.txt` list the pages. `/llms.txt` is longer than the length agents are expected to read.\n- Nothing describes the site to agents in a machine-readable way: no structured data about the project, no discovery files, no catalog of the skills the repository ships, and no dates on sitemap entries.\n- The standard's data (capabilities, edges, workflows, profiles, diagnostic codes) is available only as YAML on GitHub or as prose on the pages. There is no API and no description of one.\n- An agent cannot ask the site for Markdown at a page's own URL, a missing page answers only in HTML, and appending `.md` to a page URL returns 404.\n- There is no MCP server for the standard, and browser agents have no in-page tools.\n- There are no about, contact or privacy pages.\n\n## Objective\n\nAn agent can find the site, learn what the standard is and when to use it, read any page or data item in a machine format, and query the standard through a documented API and an MCP server, without scraping HTML. Readers' experience of the site does not get worse.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An agent can discover, from the site's own well-known locations and response headers, where the API, its description, the MCP server, the skills and the Markdown copies are.\n- [ ] The standard's capabilities, edges, workflows, domain profiles and diagnostic codes are served read-only in JSON, with a published OpenAPI description whose operations each have an id, a description and typed responses.\n- [ ] A read-only MCP server for the standard answers over the current transport, lists described tools with typed inputs and outputs, marks them read-only, and returns structured errors for bad calls.\n- [ ] A page requested with `Accept: text/markdown`, or at its URL with `.md` appended, is answered with its Markdown copy; HTML requests still get HTML. A missing page answers 404 with a short Markdown pointer to the docs when Markdown is acceptable.\n- [ ] `/llms.txt` stays within the length agents read, says when to use the standard, and links per-section files.\n- [ ] The home page carries structured data about the project and its organization, and complete social metadata; the sitemap carries last-modified dates.\n- [ ] The repository's skills are listed in an agent-skills index with a digest per skill.\n- [ ] Browser agents get read-only in-page tools for searching and reading the docs, where the browser supports them.\n- [ ] About, contact and privacy pages exist; contact goes through GitHub, and the privacy page states what the site does and does not collect.\n- [ ] Every existing site check still passes (build checks, accessibility in both themes at 1440 and 360 pixels, contrast), and a new ora.ai scan after deploy scores higher, with the remaining gaps listed.\n"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"83a4b714-3e79-496f-bd50-42903bde2540","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Cgzzv9D2y3tmnaTAbaKbb","requestRef":"17ff4b18-8d92-4fa0-a170-512cfab884fc","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"4d9d35fb-e614-4b31-9cd5-528fa07c4007","timestamp":"2026-11-13T13:23:28.602Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"feature/issue-125-site-visuals","slug":"i-just-came-across-imperative-newell"}
{"parentUuid":"4d9d35fb-e614-4b31-9cd5-528fa07c4007","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"21018bcb-25f0-462b-8a84-002bbe419b3f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012aSsYk9VFX98X2FZyPfVTF","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: REDACTED.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)"}]},"uuid":"a8667fe3-406a-487d-8f1f-fe2331f2db55","timestamp":"2026-11-13T13:23:28.777Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"create","filePath":"REDACTED.md","content":"## Context\n\nThe documentation site at acs.synapti.ai was launched under #120 and redesigned under #125. An agent-readiness scan by ora.ai (scoring version 3, scanned 2026-11-13) rates it 24/100, grade F: discovery 5/20, access 13/30, usability 4/40. Its summary is that agents can reach the site but find no API, no MCP server and no developer docs to integrate with. The standard is written for AI agents, so agents are a primary audience for the site.\n\n## Current State\n\n- Agents can read every page as Markdown at `<page>/index.md`, and `/llms.txt` and `/llms-full.txt` list the pages. `/llms.txt` is longer than the length agents are expected to read.\n- Nothing describes the site to agents in a machine-readable way: no structured data about the project, no discovery files, no catalog of the skills the repository ships, and no dates on sitemap entries.\n- The standard's data (capabilities, edges, workflows, profiles, diagnostic codes) is available only as YAML on GitHub or as prose on the pages. There is no API and no description of one.\n- An agent cannot ask the site for Markdown at a page's own URL, a missing page answers only in HTML, and appending `.md` to a page URL returns 404.\n- There is no MCP server for the standard, and browser agents have no in-page tools.\n- There are no about, contact or privacy pages.\n\n## Objective\n\nAn agent can find the site, learn what the standard is and when to use it, read any page or data item in a machine format, and query the standard through a documented API and an MCP server, without scraping HTML. Readers' experience of the site does not get worse.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] An agent can discover, from the site's own well-known locations and response headers, where the API, its description, the MCP server, the skills and the Markdown copies are.\n- [ ] The standard's capabilities, edges, workflows, domain profiles and diagnostic codes are served read-only in JSON, with a published OpenAPI description whose operations each have an id, a description and typed responses.\n- [ ] A read-only MCP server for the standard answers over the current transport, lists described tools with typed inputs and outputs, marks them read-only, and returns structured errors for bad calls.\n- [ ] A page requested with `Accept: text/markdown`, or at its URL with `.md` appended, is answered with its Markdown copy; HTML requests still get HTML. A missing page answers 404 with a short Markdown pointer to the docs when Markdown is acceptable.\n- [ ] `/llms.txt` stays within the length agents read, says when to use the standard, and links per-section files.\n- [ ] The home page carries structured data about the project and its organization, and complete social metadata; the sitemap carries last-modified dates.\n- [ ] The repository's skills are listed in an agent-skills index with a digest per skill.\n- [ ] Browser agents get read-only in-page tools for searching and reading the docs, where the browser supports them.\n- [ ] About, contact and privacy pages exist; contact goes through GitHub, and the privacy page states what the site does and does not collect.\n- [ ] Every existing site check still passes (build checks, accessibility in both themes at 1440 and 360 pixels, contrast), and a new ora.ai scan after deploy scores higher, with the remaining gaps listed.\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"4d9d35fb-e614-4b31-9cd5-528fa07c4007","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"mode"},"session_id":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"feature/issue-125-site-visuals","slug":"i-just-came-across-imperative-newell"}
