{"parentUuid":"54a28906-024d-4039-b90f-f4dbd81627af","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch113AtSEFLmp34yEbhBQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4601,"cache_read_input_tokens":567354,"output_tokens":7290,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":437},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4601,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7290,"cache_read_input_tokens":567354,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4601},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":4298,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"6e2f1ce6-bd64-43a8-8606-4004a2b58612","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011Ch1139HwpPSsqTEBQP7kh","requestRef":"24e98c97-7569-40e7-9b74-5eac7d2f2f30","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"8b77c440-b5a2-4c64-8ef4-09c56993a850","timestamp":"2026-11-13T13:24:56.002Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/i127","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"feature/issue-127-agent-ready","slug":"i-just-came-across-imperative-newell"}
{"parentUuid":"8b77c440-b5a2-4c64-8ef4-09c56993a850","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Ch113AtSEFLmp34yEbhBQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GwpGsiZmJz9LoWiUKsDR2r","name":"Workflow","input":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'site-agent-ready',\n  description: 'Issue #127: data API + OpenAPI, discovery files, Pages worker with Markdown negotiation and docs MCP server, WebMCP; integrate, review, fix',\n  phases: [{ title: 'Build' }, { title: 'Integrate' }, { title: 'Review' }, { title: 'Fix' }],\n}\nconst SP = 'REDACTED'\nconst WT = '/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/i127'\nconst COMMON = `Read first: ${SP}/agents-brief.md (contract, rules, live facts) and ${SP}/ora-checks.md (the checks). Integration worktree ${WT}, branch feature/issue-127-agent-ready.`\nconst W = (n) => `Work in a new git worktree: git -C ${WT} worktree add ${WT}/../i127-${n} -b feature/issue-127-${n} HEAD, run npm ci in its site/. Commit there; do not push. Delete that worktree's site/node_modules and site/dist when done.`\n\nconst BUILDERS = [\n  { name: 'api', task: `Build the static data API and its description, per the shared contract:\n1. Astro endpoints (or a build step) writing /api/v1/*.json from the existing loaders in site/src/data (ontology incl. capability I/O schemas, risk and flags, layers, edges, workflows with steps, profiles, diagnostics, pages index with url, markdown url, title, description, section and h2/h3 headings). Stable, documented field names; include a top-level \"version\" block (standard, ontology, plugin) and \"generated_from\" commit.\n2. /openapi.json (OpenAPI 3.1): one GET operation per endpoint, unique operationId (camelCase, verb-led e.g. listCapabilities, readCapability), summary + description, typed path params with enums where the set is known (capability ids, workflow ids, domains), full response schemas (components ok, but self-contained file), an RFC 9457 problem+json 404 response schema, info.description stating: open, no authentication, read-only, not paginated (full collections), versioned path /api/v1 with a deprecation policy (a new major path, the old one kept for at least one Standard minor version, Deprecation and Sunset headers announced first). No securitySchemes.\n3. /.well-known/api-catalog (RFC 9727): application/linkset+json; profile=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9727\" via public/_headers, linkset with anchor https://acs.synapti.ai/api/v1/ and service-desc (openapi.json), service-doc (/developers/), status? only if real.\n4. A /developers/ page (route, sidebar under a sensible section) that documents for agents and developers: the JSON API (endpoints table generated from the OpenAPI, examples with curl), the Markdown copies and llms.txt, the MCP server at https://acs.synapti.ai/mcp (tools: search_standard, read_capability, read_workflow, read_page — describe as provided by the worker builder; keep names exactly), WebMCP tools in the browser. Its title and H1 name the product and \"API\" (e.g. \"Agent Capability Standard API and agent access\"). Link it from the home page and the header or footer, and from llms.txt. Give it a Markdown copy and figures as other pages have (run-length checks!).\n5. Tests: the JSON matches the YAML sources (counts and ids read independently), the OpenAPI validates as 3.1 (write a small structural check or use a dev dependency only if necessary), every documented path exists in dist, check-dist knows the new files.` },\n  { name: 'discovery', task: `Discovery and metadata, all static:\n1. llms.txt under 30,000 chars: keep the summary, add a \"When to use this standard\" section (from the README/Standard: when an agent should consult it), the API/MCP/developer links (paths from the contract), and per-section files /<section>/llms.txt (modular) linked from the root; root keeps a short list per section. Keep llms-full.txt. Update check-dist and tests that assumed the old llms.txt.\n2. Static /llms.md and /agents.md (agents.md: how an agent should use the site: Markdown copies, API, MCP, skills; markdown with top-level heading).\n3. Markdown copies open with YAML frontmatter (--- title, description, canonical URL, last_updated from git log of the source) — check every consumer of the copies (Copy page as Markdown button, llms-full.txt, check-dist, tests) and keep code fences balanced.\n4. Sitemap lastmod per URL from the last git commit touching its source (generated pages: the data sources they read). Must not break check-dist's sitemap equality check.\n5. Home page JSON-LD (@graph): WebSite, Organization (Synapti, url https://synapti.ai, sameAs https://github.com/synaptiai; contactPoint with contactType and url of GitHub issues — no email/phone), SoftwareApplication or TechArticle/CreativeWork for the standard (name, description, url, license Apache-2.0, version from plugin.json/Standard, keywords/knowsAbout, offers price 0 USD, codeRepository via sameAs). Add og:type (website on home, article on docs pages) site-wide. Numbers from data, not typed.\n6. /.well-known/ard.json (ARD v0.91) and /.well-known/ai-catalog.json (\"specVersion\": \"1.0\") listing entries for: llms.txt, openapi.json, the MCP server (with server card URL /.well-known/mcp/server-card.json), the agent-skills index, the developers page. Each entry: urn:air:acs.synapti.ai:<name> id, displayName, media type, exactly one of url or data. Add <link rel=\"ard\" href=\"/.well-known/ard.json\"> and rel=\"ai-catalog\" on the home page. Research the ARD v0.91 and AI catalog formats on the web (WebSearch/WebFetch or a web-fetch agent) before writing; cite what you followed in the commit.\n7. /.well-known/agent-skills/index.json in the v0.2.0 schema ($schema https://schemas.agentskills.io/discovery/0.2.0/schema.json): one entry per skill in skills/*/SKILL.md (name and description from frontmatter, type \"skill-md\", url to a copy of the SKILL.md served under /.well-known/agent-skills/<name>/SKILL.md, digest sha256 of exactly the served bytes). Check the schema on the web first. Serve the copies with text/markdown. Tests recompute digests from dist.\n8. /about/, /contact/ (GitHub issues/discussions only), /privacy/ (verify first in dist: no cookies set by the site, no analytics/third-party scripts; localStorage theme preference exists — say so honestly; Cloudflare processes request logs). Each ≥500 chars, routed, with Markdown copies and passing run-length/drawn-figure checks (decide template so the checks are honest; a short trust page may need to be report-only—explain).\n9. AGENTS.md at the repo root (agents working on this repo: commands from CLAUDE.md, pointers), linked from llms.txt; add it to site routes or EXCLUSIONS as the CLAUDE.md rules require.\n10. robots.txt: keep allow-all, list the sitemap, add explicit allow groups for OAI-SearchBot, Claude-SearchBot, PerplexityBot, ChatGPT-User, Claude-User, Perplexity-User only if it changes nothing for others.\n11. Investigate /spec/1.1.0/ text size (~125K chars, check wants < ~100K): report what makes up the text (figures' hidden text versions?) and reduce it only if that does not remove information (e.g. figure text duplicated in Markdown copy only); otherwise report.` },\n  { name: 'worker', task: `The request-time layer on Cloudflare Pages, plus the docs MCP server:\n1. Research first (Cloudflare docs via the cloudflare-docs MCP tool or web): Pages Functions (site/functions) vs advanced mode (_worker.js in dist); how each interacts with public/_headers, _redirects and _routes.json; how 'wrangler pages deploy dist' (run from site/ in .github/workflows/site.yml — check its working directory) picks up functions; Free-plan invocation limits; local testing with 'npx wrangler pages dev dist'. Choose the approach that keeps static assets served without invoking code where possible (_routes.json exclude for /_astro/*, /pagefind/*, images, fonts). Write the choice and reasons in a comment at the top of the entry file.\n2. Behaviour:\n   - Accept negotiation: for an HTML page route, if the request's Accept prefers text/markdown over text/html (q-values), answer with the page's <route>index.md, Content-Type text/markdown; charset=utf-8, Vary: Accept, Link rel=canonical. HTML requests unchanged (still Vary: Accept on negotiated routes).\n   - <route-without-trailing-slash>.md (e.g. /capabilities/mutate.md, /spec/1.1.0.md) → that route's index.md. /index.md already static.\n   - AI bot user agents (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, OAI-SearchBot, Claude-SearchBot, ChatGPT-User, Claude-User, Perplexity-User) asking for a page get Markdown — decide whether that is right for search bots (they index HTML for links); the check wants GPTBot/ClaudeBot/PerplexityBot. Document the decision.\n   - 404: keep the HTML 404 page for browsers; when the client accepts text/markdown or is not a browser (no text/html in Accept), answer 404 with a short Markdown body pointing to /llms.txt, /sitemap.xml, /developers/.\n   - /api/v1/* unknown path → 404 application/problem+json (type, title, status, detail, instance), matching the OpenAPI schema the api builder writes (type URI e.g. https://acs.synapti.ai/developers/#errors).\n   - Link headers on HTML pages: rel=\"alternate\"; type=\"text/markdown\" (the page's copy), rel=\"sitemap\", rel=\"service-desc\" (/openapi.json), rel=\"api-catalog\" (/.well-known/api-catalog), rel=\"ard\"? only if registered/meaningful.\n3. Docs MCP server at /mcp: Streamable HTTP per the current MCP spec (research it: POST JSON-RPC, Accept application/json and text/event-stream, MCP-Protocol-Version header, stateless operation without sessions, GET → 405 if no SSE stream), JSON responses. initialize returns serverInfo {name, title, version (plugin/Standard version)} and instructions (purpose, start with search_standard, how tools fit). Tools (read-only; readOnlyHint true, destructiveHint false, idempotentHint true, openWorldHint false; descriptions ≥20 chars and distinct; inputSchema typed with required; self-contained outputSchema; structuredContent plus the same JSON as text): search_standard(query, limit?) over /api/v1/pages.json (+ capability names/descriptions); read_capability(id) from /api/v1/capabilities/{id}.json; read_workflow(id); read_page(path) returning the Markdown copy. Exactly these four names. Errors: JSON-RPC -32601 unknown method, -32602 invalid params/unknown tool, tool-level isError for not found. Read data via the ASSETS binding (or fetch of the same origin), no external calls. Resources: either expose a few (llms.txt, the current Standard Markdown, openapi.json) with resources/list + resources/read working, or omit the capability.\n   - Server card: /mcp/server-card and /.well-known/mcp/server-card.json ($schema https://static.modelcontextprotocol.io/schemas/v1/server-card.schema.json; name \"ai.synapti.acs/mcp\" or the reverse-DNS form the schema requires; title; description ≤100 chars; icons[].src (use the site's favicon/og image); version; remotes [{type: \"streamable-http\", url: \"https://acs.synapti.ai/mcp\"}]; no tools[]). Verify the schema on the web.\n4. Build: compile the worker/functions as part of 'npm run build' (esbuild via Vite's dependency or the wrangler bundler), so dist (or site/functions) is what deploys; update .github/workflows/site.yml only if the deploy command must change, keep the deploy gate exactly as it is.\n5. Tests: unit tests of the handler with a fake ASSETS binding (negotiation q-values, .md fallback, 404 markdown vs html, problem+json, Link headers, MCP initialize/tools/list/tools/call/errors/resources, server card). Then run it for real with 'npx wrangler pages dev dist' (or the equivalent) on 127.0.0.1 and curl every behaviour; paste the curl results in your report. Also run the official MCP inspector CLI against it if it works without a browser ('npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --cli' with tools/list) and report.` },\n  { name: 'webmcp', task: `Read-only WebMCP tools in the browser:\n1. Research the current WebMCP API (web search: document.modelContext.registerTool, navigator.modelContext fallback, tool shape: name, description, inputSchema, execute, annotations readOnlyHint/untrustedContentHint). Cite the source in a comment.\n2. A small script loaded on every page (feature-detected; does nothing without the API; no errors in browsers without it; must pass the existing CSP/axe/checks), registering: search_docs(query, limit?) using the site's Pagefind index (already built; load it lazily the same way the search dialog does), read_capability(id) and read_workflow(id) from /api/v1/... (contract), read_page_markdown(path) fetching the page's index.md. Verb-led snake_case names; descriptions within size limits; JSON Schema inputSchema with required; readOnlyHint true on all; no consequentialHint (nothing consequential). Return text content.\n3. Tests: unit tests with a fake document.modelContext checking each tool registers with valid schema and its execute returns expected content from fixtures; a check that the script is in every built page. Verify in headless Chromium that pages load without console errors (playwright-core at ~/.REDACTED, Chromium at ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/chromium-1248/chrome-mac-arm64/Google Chrome for Testing.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome for Testing), and with a stub document.modelContext injected before load that the four tools register and execute against the built site (serve dist with python3 -m http.server on 127.0.0.1).` },\n]\n\nphase('Build')\nconst built = await parallel(BUILDERS.map((b) => () => agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are builder \"${b.name}\". ${W(b.name)}\\n\\nTask:\\n${b.task}\\n\\nOther builders work in parallel on: ${BUILDERS.filter((x) => x.name !== b.name).map((x) => x.name).join(', ')} — stay inside your task; use the shared contract for anything they produce.\\n\\nReport: worktree, branch, commits, what you built, verification (commands + summary lines), not done + reason.`, { label: b.name, phase: 'Build' })))\n\nphase('Integrate')\nconst integ = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are the integrator. Builder reports:\\n${built.map((r, i) => `--- ${BUILDERS[i].name} ---\\n${r}`).join('\\n')}\\n\\nIn ${WT} (feature/issue-127-agent-ready): merge feature/issue-127-api, -discovery, -worker, -webmcp with --no-ff in that order, resolving conflicts keeping both sides (llms.txt, routes, check-dist, _headers, home page are likely shared). Make the pieces agree on the contract (API paths, MCP tool names in /developers, llms.txt, ard.json, ai-catalog.json, server card). Run all checks: in site/ npm ci, npm run check, npm test, npm run build, npm run check:axe; repo root (activate /Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.venv) pytest tests -q, python tools/validate_doc_links.py, python tools/validate_skill_refs.py. Then run the built site with the worker locally (npx wrangler pages dev dist, or as the worker builder documented) and probe every agent-facing behaviour with curl: /llms.txt length and links resolving, section llms.txt, /llms.md, /agents.md, /.well-known/ard.json, ai-catalog.json, api-catalog (content-type), agent-skills index (recompute 3 digests), mcp/server-card.json, /openapi.json, every /api/v1 endpoint, an unknown /api/v1 path (problem+json), Accept: text/markdown on / and a docs page (Vary), /capabilities/mutate.md, a 404 with Accept text/markdown and with a browser Accept, Link headers, MCP initialize + tools/list + each tool call + an error, home JSON-LD parses, sitemap lastmod, frontmatter on a copy. Fix what fails. Push normally (never force) to origin feature/issue-127-agent-ready; confirm git ls-remote equals HEAD. Remove the i127-* worktrees (keep branches). Report: merges, fixes, check results with the pushed SHA, the full probe table.`, { label: 'integrate', phase: 'Integrate' })\n\nphase('Review')\nconst review = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are an independent reviewer; you built none of this. Integrator report:\\n${integ}\\n\\nAt the pushed head of feature/issue-127-agent-ready in ${WT}: (a) code review of git diff origin/main...HEAD for correctness, security (the worker is internet-facing: path traversal in .md/route mapping, header injection, unbounded inputs to search, JSON-RPC body size, CORS choices), error handling, and whether anything fakes a capability; (b) run the site with the worker locally and act as an agent: discover from / only (headers, link tags, llms.txt, well-known files) and get to the API, the MCP server and a capability's data without prior knowledge; use the MCP server through a real client if possible (npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --cli, or the TypeScript SDK client) — tools/list, each tool, bad args; check the OpenAPI against the JSON actually served (validate 3 responses against their schemas); check WebMCP registration in headless Chromium with a stub; (c) check readers' experience did not regress: screenshots at 1440 light/dark and 360 of home, /developers/, /about/, /privacy/, a capability page — OPEN them; save under ${SP}/visual/i127/review/. Do not edit repository files. Report findings P1/P2/P3 with file:line, trigger, and the change.`, { label: 'review', phase: 'Review' })\n\nphase('Fix')\nconst fixed = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nFix every finding of this review in ${WT} on feature/issue-127-agent-ready at the pushed head:\\n${review}\\n\\nP1, P2 and P3; unfixed only with a concrete reason (a maintainer decision, or a check that does not apply). Each P1/P2 fix gets a test that fails without it. Re-run all checks the integrator ran and the local worker probe for what you changed; push normally; confirm ls-remote equals HEAD. Report: per finding what was done (commit), checks with the pushed SHA, the final probe table, remaining gaps against ${SP}/ora-checks.md (which checks will still fail and why).`, { label: 'fix', phase: 'Fix' })\n\nreturn { built, integ, review, fixed }\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4601,"cache_read_input_tokens":567354,"output_tokens":7290,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":437},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4601,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":7290,"cache_read_input_tokens":567354,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4601,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4601},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"toolInputStreamMs":66849,"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GwpGsiZmJz9LoWiUKsDR2r":{"script":"export const meta = {\n  name: 'site-agent-ready',\n  description: 'Issue #127: data API + OpenAPI, discovery files, Pages worker with Markdown negotiation and docs MCP server, WebMCP; integrate, review, fix',\n  phases: [{ title: 'Build' }, { title: 'Integrate' }, { title: 'Review' }, { title: 'Fix' }],\n}\nconst SP = 'REDACTED'\nconst WT = '/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/i127'\nconst COMMON = `Read first: ${SP}/agents-brief.md (contract, rules, live facts) and ${SP}/ora-checks.md (the checks). Integration worktree ${WT}, branch feature/issue-127-agent-ready.`\nconst W = (n) => `Work in a new git worktree: git -C ${WT} worktree add ${WT}/../i127-${n} -b feature/issue-127-${n} HEAD, run npm ci in its site/. Commit there; do not push. Delete that worktree's site/node_modules and site/dist when done.`\n\nconst BUILDERS = [\n  { name: 'api', task: `Build the static data API and its description, per the shared contract:\n1. Astro endpoints (or a build step) writing /api/v1/*.json from the existing loaders in site/src/data (ontology incl. capability I/O schemas, risk and flags, layers, edges, workflows with steps, profiles, diagnostics, pages index with url, markdown url, title, description, section and h2/h3 headings). Stable, documented field names; include a top-level \"version\" block (standard, ontology, plugin) and \"generated_from\" commit.\n2. /openapi.json (OpenAPI 3.1): one GET operation per endpoint, unique operationId (camelCase, verb-led e.g. listCapabilities, readCapability), summary + description, typed path params with enums where the set is known (capability ids, workflow ids, domains), full response schemas (components ok, but self-contained file), an RFC 9457 problem+json 404 response schema, info.description stating: open, no authentication, read-only, not paginated (full collections), versioned path /api/v1 with a deprecation policy (a new major path, the old one kept for at least one Standard minor version, Deprecation and Sunset headers announced first). No securitySchemes.\n3. /.well-known/api-catalog (RFC 9727): application/linkset+json; profile=\"https://www.rfc-editor.org/info/rfc9727\" via public/_headers, linkset with anchor https://acs.synapti.ai/api/v1/ and service-desc (openapi.json), service-doc (/developers/), status? only if real.\n4. A /developers/ page (route, sidebar under a sensible section) that documents for agents and developers: the JSON API (endpoints table generated from the OpenAPI, examples with curl), the Markdown copies and llms.txt, the MCP server at https://acs.synapti.ai/mcp (tools: search_standard, read_capability, read_workflow, read_page — describe as provided by the worker builder; keep names exactly), WebMCP tools in the browser. Its title and H1 name the product and \"API\" (e.g. \"Agent Capability Standard API and agent access\"). Link it from the home page and the header or footer, and from llms.txt. Give it a Markdown copy and figures as other pages have (run-length checks!).\n5. Tests: the JSON matches the YAML sources (counts and ids read independently), the OpenAPI validates as 3.1 (write a small structural check or use a dev dependency only if necessary), every documented path exists in dist, check-dist knows the new files.` },\n  { name: 'discovery', task: `Discovery and metadata, all static:\n1. llms.txt under 30,000 chars: keep the summary, add a \"When to use this standard\" section (from the README/Standard: when an agent should consult it), the API/MCP/developer links (paths from the contract), and per-section files /<section>/llms.txt (modular) linked from the root; root keeps a short list per section. Keep llms-full.txt. Update check-dist and tests that assumed the old llms.txt.\n2. Static /llms.md and /agents.md (agents.md: how an agent should use the site: Markdown copies, API, MCP, skills; markdown with top-level heading).\n3. Markdown copies open with YAML frontmatter (--- title, description, canonical URL, last_updated from git log of the source) — check every consumer of the copies (Copy page as Markdown button, llms-full.txt, check-dist, tests) and keep code fences balanced.\n4. Sitemap lastmod per URL from the last git commit touching its source (generated pages: the data sources they read). Must not break check-dist's sitemap equality check.\n5. Home page JSON-LD (@graph): WebSite, Organization (Synapti, url https://synapti.ai, sameAs https://github.com/synaptiai; contactPoint with contactType and url of GitHub issues — no email/phone), SoftwareApplication or TechArticle/CreativeWork for the standard (name, description, url, license Apache-2.0, version from plugin.json/Standard, keywords/knowsAbout, offers price 0 USD, codeRepository via sameAs). Add og:type (website on home, article on docs pages) site-wide. Numbers from data, not typed.\n6. /.well-known/ard.json (ARD v0.91) and /.well-known/ai-catalog.json (\"specVersion\": \"1.0\") listing entries for: llms.txt, openapi.json, the MCP server (with server card URL /.well-known/mcp/server-card.json), the agent-skills index, the developers page. Each entry: urn:air:acs.synapti.ai:<name> id, displayName, media type, exactly one of url or data. Add <link rel=\"ard\" href=\"/.well-known/ard.json\"> and rel=\"ai-catalog\" on the home page. Research the ARD v0.91 and AI catalog formats on the web (WebSearch/WebFetch or a web-fetch agent) before writing; cite what you followed in the commit.\n7. /.well-known/agent-skills/index.json in the v0.2.0 schema ($schema https://schemas.agentskills.io/discovery/0.2.0/schema.json): one entry per skill in skills/*/SKILL.md (name and description from frontmatter, type \"skill-md\", url to a copy of the SKILL.md served under /.well-known/agent-skills/<name>/SKILL.md, digest sha256 of exactly the served bytes). Check the schema on the web first. Serve the copies with text/markdown. Tests recompute digests from dist.\n8. /about/, /contact/ (GitHub issues/discussions only), /privacy/ (verify first in dist: no cookies set by the site, no analytics/third-party scripts; localStorage theme preference exists — say so honestly; Cloudflare processes request logs). Each ≥500 chars, routed, with Markdown copies and passing run-length/drawn-figure checks (decide template so the checks are honest; a short trust page may need to be report-only—explain).\n9. AGENTS.md at the repo root (agents working on this repo: commands from CLAUDE.md, pointers), linked from llms.txt; add it to site routes or EXCLUSIONS as the CLAUDE.md rules require.\n10. robots.txt: keep allow-all, list the sitemap, add explicit allow groups for OAI-SearchBot, Claude-SearchBot, PerplexityBot, ChatGPT-User, Claude-User, Perplexity-User only if it changes nothing for others.\n11. Investigate /spec/1.1.0/ text size (~125K chars, check wants < ~100K): report what makes up the text (figures' hidden text versions?) and reduce it only if that does not remove information (e.g. figure text duplicated in Markdown copy only); otherwise report.` },\n  { name: 'worker', task: `The request-time layer on Cloudflare Pages, plus the docs MCP server:\n1. Research first (Cloudflare docs via the cloudflare-docs MCP tool or web): Pages Functions (site/functions) vs advanced mode (_worker.js in dist); how each interacts with public/_headers, _redirects and _routes.json; how 'wrangler pages deploy dist' (run from site/ in .github/workflows/site.yml — check its working directory) picks up functions; Free-plan invocation limits; local testing with 'npx wrangler pages dev dist'. Choose the approach that keeps static assets served without invoking code where possible (_routes.json exclude for /_astro/*, /pagefind/*, images, fonts). Write the choice and reasons in a comment at the top of the entry file.\n2. Behaviour:\n   - Accept negotiation: for an HTML page route, if the request's Accept prefers text/markdown over text/html (q-values), answer with the page's <route>index.md, Content-Type text/markdown; charset=utf-8, Vary: Accept, Link rel=canonical. HTML requests unchanged (still Vary: Accept on negotiated routes).\n   - <route-without-trailing-slash>.md (e.g. /capabilities/mutate.md, /spec/1.1.0.md) → that route's index.md. /index.md already static.\n   - AI bot user agents (GPTBot, ClaudeBot, PerplexityBot, OAI-SearchBot, Claude-SearchBot, ChatGPT-User, Claude-User, Perplexity-User) asking for a page get Markdown — decide whether that is right for search bots (they index HTML for links); the check wants GPTBot/ClaudeBot/PerplexityBot. Document the decision.\n   - 404: keep the HTML 404 page for browsers; when the client accepts text/markdown or is not a browser (no text/html in Accept), answer 404 with a short Markdown body pointing to /llms.txt, /sitemap.xml, /developers/.\n   - /api/v1/* unknown path → 404 application/problem+json (type, title, status, detail, instance), matching the OpenAPI schema the api builder writes (type URI e.g. https://acs.synapti.ai/developers/#errors).\n   - Link headers on HTML pages: rel=\"alternate\"; type=\"text/markdown\" (the page's copy), rel=\"sitemap\", rel=\"service-desc\" (/openapi.json), rel=\"api-catalog\" (/.well-known/api-catalog), rel=\"ard\"? only if registered/meaningful.\n3. Docs MCP server at /mcp: Streamable HTTP per the current MCP spec (research it: POST JSON-RPC, Accept application/json and text/event-stream, MCP-Protocol-Version header, stateless operation without sessions, GET → 405 if no SSE stream), JSON responses. initialize returns serverInfo {name, title, version (plugin/Standard version)} and instructions (purpose, start with search_standard, how tools fit). Tools (read-only; readOnlyHint true, destructiveHint false, idempotentHint true, openWorldHint false; descriptions ≥20 chars and distinct; inputSchema typed with required; self-contained outputSchema; structuredContent plus the same JSON as text): search_standard(query, limit?) over /api/v1/pages.json (+ capability names/descriptions); read_capability(id) from /api/v1/capabilities/{id}.json; read_workflow(id); read_page(path) returning the Markdown copy. Exactly these four names. Errors: JSON-RPC -32601 unknown method, -32602 invalid params/unknown tool, tool-level isError for not found. Read data via the ASSETS binding (or fetch of the same origin), no external calls. Resources: either expose a few (llms.txt, the current Standard Markdown, openapi.json) with resources/list + resources/read working, or omit the capability.\n   - Server card: /mcp/server-card and /.well-known/mcp/server-card.json ($schema https://static.modelcontextprotocol.io/schemas/v1/server-card.schema.json; name \"ai.synapti.acs/mcp\" or the reverse-DNS form the schema requires; title; description ≤100 chars; icons[].src (use the site's favicon/og image); version; remotes [{type: \"streamable-http\", url: \"https://acs.synapti.ai/mcp\"}]; no tools[]). Verify the schema on the web.\n4. Build: compile the worker/functions as part of 'npm run build' (esbuild via Vite's dependency or the wrangler bundler), so dist (or site/functions) is what deploys; update .github/workflows/site.yml only if the deploy command must change, keep the deploy gate exactly as it is.\n5. Tests: unit tests of the handler with a fake ASSETS binding (negotiation q-values, .md fallback, 404 markdown vs html, problem+json, Link headers, MCP initialize/tools/list/tools/call/errors/resources, server card). Then run it for real with 'npx wrangler pages dev dist' (or the equivalent) on 127.0.0.1 and curl every behaviour; paste the curl results in your report. Also run the official MCP inspector CLI against it if it works without a browser ('npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --cli' with tools/list) and report.` },\n  { name: 'webmcp', task: `Read-only WebMCP tools in the browser:\n1. Research the current WebMCP API (web search: document.modelContext.registerTool, navigator.modelContext fallback, tool shape: name, description, inputSchema, execute, annotations readOnlyHint/untrustedContentHint). Cite the source in a comment.\n2. A small script loaded on every page (feature-detected; does nothing without the API; no errors in browsers without it; must pass the existing CSP/axe/checks), registering: search_docs(query, limit?) using the site's Pagefind index (already built; load it lazily the same way the search dialog does), read_capability(id) and read_workflow(id) from /api/v1/... (contract), read_page_markdown(path) fetching the page's index.md. Verb-led snake_case names; descriptions within size limits; JSON Schema inputSchema with required; readOnlyHint true on all; no consequentialHint (nothing consequential). Return text content.\n3. Tests: unit tests with a fake document.modelContext checking each tool registers with valid schema and its execute returns expected content from fixtures; a check that the script is in every built page. Verify in headless Chromium that pages load without console errors (playwright-core at ~/.REDACTED, Chromium at ~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright/chromium-1248/chrome-mac-arm64/Google Chrome for Testing.app/Contents/MacOS/Google Chrome for Testing), and with a stub document.modelContext injected before load that the four tools register and execute against the built site (serve dist with python3 -m http.server on 127.0.0.1).` },\n]\n\nphase('Build')\nconst built = await parallel(BUILDERS.map((b) => () => agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are builder \"${b.name}\". ${W(b.name)}\\n\\nTask:\\n${b.task}\\n\\nOther builders work in parallel on: ${BUILDERS.filter((x) => x.name !== b.name).map((x) => x.name).join(', ')} — stay inside your task; use the shared contract for anything they produce.\\n\\nReport: worktree, branch, commits, what you built, verification (commands + summary lines), not done + reason.`, { label: b.name, phase: 'Build' })))\n\nphase('Integrate')\nconst integ = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are the integrator. Builder reports:\\n${built.map((r, i) => `--- ${BUILDERS[i].name} ---\\n${r}`).join('\\n')}\\n\\nIn ${WT} (feature/issue-127-agent-ready): merge feature/issue-127-api, -discovery, -worker, -webmcp with --no-ff in that order, resolving conflicts keeping both sides (llms.txt, routes, check-dist, _headers, home page are likely shared). Make the pieces agree on the contract (API paths, MCP tool names in /developers, llms.txt, ard.json, ai-catalog.json, server card). Run all checks: in site/ npm ci, npm run check, npm test, npm run build, npm run check:axe; repo root (activate /Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.venv) pytest tests -q, python tools/validate_doc_links.py, python tools/validate_skill_refs.py. Then run the built site with the worker locally (npx wrangler pages dev dist, or as the worker builder documented) and probe every agent-facing behaviour with curl: /llms.txt length and links resolving, section llms.txt, /llms.md, /agents.md, /.well-known/ard.json, ai-catalog.json, api-catalog (content-type), agent-skills index (recompute 3 digests), mcp/server-card.json, /openapi.json, every /api/v1 endpoint, an unknown /api/v1 path (problem+json), Accept: text/markdown on / and a docs page (Vary), /capabilities/mutate.md, a 404 with Accept text/markdown and with a browser Accept, Link headers, MCP initialize + tools/list + each tool call + an error, home JSON-LD parses, sitemap lastmod, frontmatter on a copy. Fix what fails. Push normally (never force) to origin feature/issue-127-agent-ready; confirm git ls-remote equals HEAD. Remove the i127-* worktrees (keep branches). Report: merges, fixes, check results with the pushed SHA, the full probe table.`, { label: 'integrate', phase: 'Integrate' })\n\nphase('Review')\nconst review = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nYou are an independent reviewer; you built none of this. Integrator report:\\n${integ}\\n\\nAt the pushed head of feature/issue-127-agent-ready in ${WT}: (a) code review of git diff origin/main...HEAD for correctness, security (the worker is internet-facing: path traversal in .md/route mapping, header injection, unbounded inputs to search, JSON-RPC body size, CORS choices), error handling, and whether anything fakes a capability; (b) run the site with the worker locally and act as an agent: discover from / only (headers, link tags, llms.txt, well-known files) and get to the API, the MCP server and a capability's data without prior knowledge; use the MCP server through a real client if possible (npx @modelcontextprotocol/inspector --cli, or the TypeScript SDK client) — tools/list, each tool, bad args; check the OpenAPI against the JSON actually served (validate 3 responses against their schemas); check WebMCP registration in headless Chromium with a stub; (c) check readers' experience did not regress: screenshots at 1440 light/dark and 360 of home, /developers/, /about/, /privacy/, a capability page — OPEN them; save under ${SP}/visual/i127/review/. Do not edit repository files. Report findings P1/P2/P3 with file:line, trigger, and the change.`, { label: 'review', phase: 'Review' })\n\nphase('Fix')\nconst fixed = await agent(`${COMMON}\\n\\nFix every finding of this review in ${WT} on feature/issue-127-agent-ready at the pushed head:\\n${review}\\n\\nP1, P2 and P3; unfixed only with a concrete reason (a maintainer decision, or a check that does not apply). Each P1/P2 fix gets a test that fails without it. Re-run all checks the integrator ran and the local worker probe for what you changed; push normally; confirm ls-remote equals HEAD. Report: per finding what was done (commit), checks with the pushed SHA, the final probe table, remaining gaps against ${SP}/ora-checks.md (which checks will still fail and why).`, { label: 'fix', phase: 'Fix' })\n\nreturn { built, integ, review, fixed }\n"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"6e2f1ce6-bd64-43a8-8606-4004a2b58612","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Ch1139HwpPSsqTEBQP7kh","requestRef":"24e98c97-7569-40e7-9b74-5eac7d2f2f30","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","promptCacheRequested":"1h","type":"assistant","uuid":"43277dcf-0176-4d93-a6bd-c8b6edd22e3c","timestamp":"2026-11-13T13:26:02.863Z","advisorModel":"claude-fable-5-2","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/danielbentes/agent-capability-standard/.claude/worktrees/i127","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","version":"2.1.322","gitBranch":"feature/issue-127-agent-ready","slug":"i-just-came-across-imperative-newell"}
