{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-13T15:50:10.245Z","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a9feb9430ac0778cd</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01N4qcYa9BinZxPU7H66q6cu</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>REDACTED.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Code review of PR diff\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>I found no P1 or P2 issues. There are six P3 findings, so the recommendation is **COMMENT**. The worker, MCP server, tools and WebMCP code are sound overall. What remains is in the search ranking, `read_page`, one routing inconsistency at `/spec/`, and the MCP server's origin and error handling.\n\n**What the evidence is.** I reviewed `origin/main` (a4c00cd) against HEAD 11ef8cc. The worker and tool code was run from HEAD source in a scratch vitest harness. The files it served came from a fake ASSETS backed by `site/dist`, which was built at 83372b0. Since that build only the worker, `redirects.ts` and `worker-build.ts` and `worker.test.ts` have changed, so page and API data from `dist` is current. The fake ASSETS does not reproduce Cloudflare's 308s, 304s or `_headers`, so HIGH applies only to logic inside the worker and tools. `test/worker.test.ts` and `test/webmcp.test.ts` pass (106 tests).\n\n## Code Review Findings\n\n### P1 — Critical (Blocks Merge)\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n\n### P2 — Should Fix\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n\n### P3 — Consider\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **F1 · correctness · `site/src/worker/tools.ts:268-274`**&lt;br&gt;`scorePage` matches every term as a substring (`title.includes(t)`, `path.includes(t)`, `desc.includes(t)`, `heads.includes(t)`), whatever its length. `searchTerms` falls back to all-stopword queries, so one- and two-letter terms reach these checks. Measured results: `\"e\"` matched 172 of 172 pages, `\"what is it\"` matched 171, and `\"x\"` matched 114 (each scoring 19, mostly from \"examples\", \"taxonomy\", \"annex\"). All three reported `matched: \"all\"`. _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: `search_standard {query:\"e\"}` or any query made only of stopwords → nearly every page is returned as a full match, ranked by noise. | Match whole stemmed words: compare against `words(x).map(stem)` for title, description, headings and section, as is already done for `titleWords`. Or keep substring matching only for terms of 3 or more characters. When every term is a stopword, return a `failed()` asking for content words rather than searching on them. |\n| **F2 · correctness · `site/src/worker/tools.ts:275`**&lt;br&gt;The +10 id bonus `stem(record.id.toLowerCase()) === t` can never fire for an id that contains `_` or `-`. `words()` (`terms.ts:9`) splits on those characters, so no term ever equals `debug_code_change`. In the probe, `\"debug_code_change\"` became terms `[debug, code, chang]`. The workflow still ranked first (score 31), but only on title and path; the comment at `tools.ts:243` says the id counts most. The bonus works today only for capabilities, because every capability id is a single word. _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: `search_standard` with any workflow id → the id bonus never applies. | Compare the id's stemmed words with the terms (for example, add the bonus when every word of `words(record.id).map(stem)` is in `terms`), or compare against `phrase` with `_` replaced by spaces. Add a test whose expected score includes the id bonus for a multi-word workflow id. |\n| **F3 · correctness · `site/src/worker/page-text.ts:74`**&lt;br&gt;`pagePart` takes the first heading whose text matches `section` (`findIndex`). Headings repeat on 44 of the built Markdown copies (dist at 83372b0, matching heading lines outside code fences, case ignored). For example, `/capabilities/mutate/` has `### Inputs` under both `## Contract` and `## Claude Code skill`. `read_page {section:\"Inputs\"}` silently returns the first; the second is reachable only by paging with offsets. The WebMCP `read_page` uses the same function (`webmcp.ts:275`). _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: `read_page {path:\"/capabilities/mutate/\", section:\"Inputs\"}` → returns the Contract inputs; the skill's inputs cannot be selected. | Accept a parent path such as `\"Claude Code skill &gt; Inputs\"`, or report the ambiguity: return the first match plus a note listing the other matching headings and their offsets. |\n| **F4 · correctness · `site/src/lib/copy-frontmatter.ts:17` and `site/src/worker/site.ts:88`**&lt;br&gt;`/spec/` is both a route (`routes.ts:230`, listed in `pages.json`) and a 302 to `/spec/1.1.0/` that the worker applies before any asset lookup (`site.ts:191`). The probe showed the two documented ways to get \"the Markdown of `/spec/`\" give different documents. `GET /spec/` with `Accept: text/markdown` → 302 → the Standard. `GET /spec.md` and `/spec/index.md` → 200, the \"Specification\" overview listing. That listing's front matter `canonical` and its `Link: rel=\"canonical\"` header both name `https://acs.synapti.ai/spec/`, a URL that serves the Standard. `read_page {path:\"/spec/\"}` also returns `url: https://acs.synapti.ai/spec/` for the listing. The redirect itself is older than this branch; the canonical, the `.md` alias and the `read_page` URL are new. _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: an agent or crawler reads `/spec/index.md` or `/spec.md` and follows its canonical → lands on a different document. | Either give the overview its own URL that does not redirect, or have the `canonical` and `read_page` `url` for a redirected route name the redirect target. Also make `/spec.md` and `/spec/index.md` follow the same redirect as `/spec/`. |\n| **F5 · correctness · `site/src/worker/mcp.ts:72-80`, `:251`**&lt;br&gt;`validOrigin` accepts only `http:` and `https:`. The probe showed `Origin: chrome-extension://abcdef`, `moz-extension://x` and `vscode-webview://x` all get 403. These are valid serialized origins, and they are what a browser-extension or VS Code webview MCP client sends. This contradicts the file header (`mcp.ts:16-20`), which says every origin may call the server and only a header that \"is not an origin at all\" is refused. _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: an MCP client running in a browser extension POSTs to `/mcp` → 403, although the server is public and allows CORS `*`. | Accept any value that parses as a URL whose `origin` is not `\"null\"` and equals the header, or simply accept every syntactically valid `scheme://host[:port]`. Keep the 403 only for values that are not origins. Add a test for an extension origin. |\n| **F6 · error-handling · `site/src/worker/mcp.ts:300-305`, `:192`**&lt;br&gt;A request whose `MCP-Protocol-Version` header is neither a legacy version nor a known one, and which has no `_meta` version, is classed as modern and strictly checked. It then fails with `-32602 params._meta[\"io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion\"] is required`. The real problem is an unsupported protocol version, which `-32022` (`mcp.ts:198`) already describes. _(MEDIUM)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: `POST /mcp` with header `MCP-Protocol-Version: 2024-13-01` and method `tools/list` → a 400 that names the wrong cause. | When the header is present, is not in `SUPPORTED_VERSIONS`, and the body has no `_meta` version, throw the `-32022` \"Unsupported protocol version\" error with `supported`/`requested` before `checkModern`'s `_meta` check. |\n\n### Summary\n- **Spec compliance (issue #127).**\n  - Met as far as the code shows: discovery (well-known files, `Link` headers), the JSON API with OpenAPI, the read-only MCP server with typed tools and structured errors, Markdown negotiation with `.md` and a Markdown 404, the per-section llms.txt files, structured data and sitemap `lastmod`, the agent-skills index with digests, the in-page WebMCP tools, and the about, contact and privacy pages.\n  - Not checkable from code: \"every existing site check still passes\" (I did not run the build or the accessibility runs) and \"a new ora.ai scan scores higher\".\n  - Interpreted: \"Markdown is acceptable\" is implemented as \"Accept does not name `text/html` exactly\".\n- **Files reviewed:** all of `site/src/worker/*`, `webmcp.ts`, `WebMcp.astro`, `api.ts`, `api-pages.ts`, `agent-contract.ts`, `agent-skills.ts`, `page-dates.ts`, `structured-data.ts`, `redirects.ts`, `worker-build.ts`, `copy-frontmatter.ts`, the `routes.ts`, `DocLayout.astro` and `agent-files.ts` diffs, and `test/json-schema-lite.ts`. `openapi.ts` and `check-dist.mjs` were read in part. **Not read:** `developers.ts` (beyond its claims about errors and negotiation), `pages/developers/index.astro`, `twins/developers.ts`, `resource-catalog.ts` past its header, and every test file except `json-schema-lite.ts`. I did not check whether the expected values in `worker.test.ts` are copied from the implementation.\n- **Callers examined** (git grep; the language server was not used): `redirectsFile` 3 (astro.config.mjs; signature unchanged), `robotsTxt` 2, `sitemapXml` 2, `llmsTxt` 3, `withStaticRules` 7, `pagePart` 4.\n- **Blast radius:** `flow-contract-files.sh` found no contract files (exit 1 after a successful `git diff`). `openapi.json` is generated by `openapi.ts`, and `check-dist.mjs` checks that every file under `/api/v1/` matches the OpenAPI paths.\n- **Clone scan:** STATE=unavailable. REASON=jscpd is not installed, so no clone scan was performed. INSTALL=`npm install -g jscpd@5.3.1`. Copy-paste duplication was therefore not checked.\n- **Candidates examined:**\n  - 1 for `parseAccept`: none exists elsewhere.\n  - 2 for `words`/`stem` (`search-match.ts`'s `wordsAt` does something different).\n  - 2 for `markdownHeadings` (`anchors.ts` `computeAnchors`). It is a second heading parser, but `page-text.ts` must have no imports so it can go into the worker bundle and the browser script, so I did not raise it as a finding.\n- **Checked and not a problem:**\n  - A full search reads about 965 KB of JSON (`pages.json` 380 KB, `workflows.json` 460 KB, `capabilities.json` 126 KB) and took 3.4 ms per call locally (Node, warm). That is not a measurement on Workers.\n  - `page-dates.ts` refuses shallow clones, and the only job that builds the site (`site.yml`) checks out with `fetch-depth: 0`.\n  - The JSON-LD escapes `&lt;`.\n  - The `ref-errors` anchor exists on the developers page.\n  - All 36 capability ids and 28 workflow ids match the tool id patterns.\n- **Total findings:** P1: 0, P2: 0, P3: 6.\n- **Recommendation:** COMMENT.\n\nThe probe harness is at `REDACTED.test.ts`.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>167414</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>40</tool_uses><duration_ms>558364</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
