{"type":"queue-operation","operation":"enqueue","timestamp":"2026-11-13T16:11:36.642Z","sessionId":"ef9f3fc8-12b6-4fe4-9f49-aaeedec05648","content":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>a27cd89d998ee9afb</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01JJLeCccDenYZ466Zxto4k2</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>REDACTED.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Agent \"Re-review review-fix commits\" finished</summary>\n<note>A task-notification fires each time this agent stops with no live background children of its own. The user can send it another message and resume it, so the same task-id may notify more than once.</note>\n<result>## Code Review Findings: re-review of `11ef8cc..54dc9fc`\n\nI found two P2 regressions and no P1s. The decision is REQUEST_CHANGES.\n\n### P1 — Critical (Blocks Merge)\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n\n### P2 — Should Fix\n| Finding | Suggested Fix |\n|---------|---------------|\n| **F1 · correctness · `site/src/worker/tools.ts:260`, `:275-280`**&lt;br&gt;The switch from substring matching to whole-stem matching breaks plural queries. The stemmer is inconsistent for words ending in `-y`: `stem(\"verifies\")` gives `verif`, but `stem(\"verify\")` stays `verify`. The same happens with `policies`/`policy` and `capabilities`/`capability`. The old substring test hid this, because `verif` is contained in `verify`. With exact `Set.has` the plural query no longer matches the singular word in titles, paths, descriptions or headings. I ran the full `search_standard` tool from 11ef8cc and from 54dc9fc against the built site data. `verifies` went from 63 results to 4, and `/capabilities/verify/` and `/layers/verify/` are gone. `policies` went from 17 to 4, and `/compliance/iso-42001/ai-policy-template/` is gone. `capabilities` went from 104 to 82. The singular queries `verify` and `policy` return the same totals as before (20 and 18). _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: an agent searches for a plural ending in `-ies` (e.g. \"verifies\", \"policies\", \"capabilities\") → pages titled or pathed with the singular drop out. | Make the stemmer map `-ies` and `-y` to the same stem, e.g. `ies → y` followed by a final `y → i`, or strip a trailing `y` when the remainder is ≥4 characters. Keep this in `terms.ts` so the build's `search_terms` and the worker stay in step. Rebuild `pages.json`. Add a test where a plural query finds the singular title (`verifies` → `/capabilities/verify/`). |\n| **F2 · correctness · `site/src/worker/page-text.ts:82`**&lt;br&gt;The `section` argument is now always split on `&gt;`, so a heading that contains `&gt;` can no longer be selected. In the built site, `/background/research/validator-engine-disagreements-1-0/` has the heading \"D11. A map&lt;K,V&gt; annotation hides the whole reference from the engine\" (source: `docs/research/analysis/validator-engine-disagreements-1.0.md:1122`). I ran `pagePart` with that exact heading text and it returned \"The page has no section headed …\". The error then lists that same heading as a valid choice, so the agent is told to use a name that fails. Before the commit, the exact text matched. I scanned all 216 built Markdown files and this is the only such heading today. The built site predates the commit (16:53 vs 17:06), but the heading is in the source, so the result holds. _(HIGH)_&lt;br&gt;Trigger: `read_page` (MCP or WebMCP) with `section` set to any heading containing `&gt;` → \"no section\" error. | Try the whole trimmed string as one heading first, and fall back to the path form only when that misses. Or split only on `/\\s+&gt;\\s+/`, which \"map&lt;K,V&gt;\" does not match. Add that heading to a test. |\n\n### Checked and not reported\n- **Missing-file reads now throw `ReadFailed` (`data.ts`).** Capability and workflow ids are checked against `CAPABILITY_RE`/`WORKFLOW_RE` before the read. `dist/404.html` exists, so a missing `.json` returns 404 and still produces \"no such id\", not -32603.\n- **`existsSync` check in `page-dates.ts:93`.** `pageSources` throws for none of the 172 routes in `ROUTES`, which is the list the sitemap build uses.\n- **Multi-word id bonus (`tools.ts:269-270`).** It never applies to `monitor_and_replan`, because the query drops the stopword \"and\" while `idWords` keeps it. I confirmed this with a stub page. It is not a regression: the old exact-id match could not fire for any multi-word id either. On real data, `/workflows/monitor-and-replan/` still ranks first for \"monitor and replan\". It is still a gap in what the commit message claims; dropping stopwords from `idWords` would close it.\n- **Other changes, read and found correct:** `validOrigin` (any scheme with a host; default ports and case handled), the `checkModern` -32022 path, `markdownRedirect` (`/spec.md` and `/spec/index.md` go to `/spec/1.1.0/index.md`; `/api/v1/` redirects to a non-page and returns null), `redirectedPage` in search and `read_page`, the 503 fallback in `site.ts`, WebMCP's `limit` rejection and `allSettled`, and the `agent-skills` YAML guards.\n- **Tests:** `npx vitest run` on `page-dates`, `worker`, `webmcp` and `agent-skills` passed 133 of 133 at 54dc9fc. None of them covers F1 or F2.\n\n### Summary\n- Files reviewed: 10 source files in the diff (tests read through the run).\n- Callers examined:\n  - `SiteData.file`/`json`/`text`: 6 (grep).\n  - `scorePage`: 1 (grep).\n  - `pagePart`: 2, the MCP and WebMCP `read_page` (grep).\n  - `validOrigin`: 1 (grep).\n- Clone scan: not run. I reviewed only the given commit range by hand.\n- Candidates examined: 2 for `markdownRedirect`/`redirectedPage`, against `redirectTarget`. They wrap it rather than copy it.\n- Total findings: P1: 0, P2: 2, P3: not counted (as asked).\n- Recommendation: REQUEST_CHANGES.\n\nThe comparison and probe scripts are in `REDACTED`:\n- `cmp.mts`: old vs new search on the built data.\n- `scan.mts`: heading reachability across the built Markdown.\n- `pd.mts`: `pageSources` over every route.\n- `id.mts`: the id-bonus probe.</result>\n<usage><subagent_tokens>80627</subagent_tokens><tool_uses>29</tool_uses><duration_ms>306640</duration_ms></usage>\n</task-notification>"}
